/* Generated by tools/build_devo.js. Long form daily teaching and prayers with exact KJV scripture. */
window.DEVO = {"anxiety":{"doc":["Anxiety is not a modern invention. The Bible meets it head on, and its answer is deeper than \"just stop worrying.\" The Greek word Jesus and Paul use, merimnao, comes from two ideas: to divide, and the mind. Anxiety is a divided mind, pulled in different directions by things that have not happened yet. That is why Jesus says, \"Take no thought for your life\" (Matthew 6:25): He is not telling you to stop caring, He is telling you not to let your mind be torn into pieces.","The doctrine underneath is the Fatherhood and providence of God. Jesus does not argue from willpower; He argues from the character of the Father. If He feeds the birds and clothes the grass, and you are worth far more than they are, then your worry is a quiet verdict that you do not believe He is that kind of Father (Matthew 6:26-32). Anxiety is therefore more than a feeling: it is a question about who is in charge. Providence means that God governs all things for the good of those who love Him (Romans 8:28), and that no sparrow falls without Him.","Paul gives the practice: \"in every thing by prayer and supplication with thanksgiving let your requests be made known unto God\" (Philippians 4:6). Notice the order. You name the thing (requests), you hand it over (prayer), and you remember who He has been (thanksgiving). The result is not always a changed situation. It is a guard: the peace of God \"shall keep your hearts and minds.\" The word for keep is a military word, a garrison posted at the gate. Peace does not remove the battle; it stands guard inside you while the battle goes on.","Peter adds the verb: \"casting all your care upon him\" (1 Peter 5:7). To cast is to throw something onto another's back, the way you would throw a cloak over a donkey. You are not asked to hold the care more gently; you are asked to throw it, and let Him carry it, \"for he careth for you.\""],"christ":"Revealed in Christ: Jesus is the one who slept in the stern of a boat in a storm (Mark 4:38). The Son of God was not anxious, because He knew the Father. In Gethsemane He felt real agony (Luke 22:44), so He is not a distant teacher but a High Priest who can be touched with the feeling of our infirmities (Hebrews 4:15). He took anxiety to the Father in prayer, and He teaches you to do the same.","word":"The word behind the word: \"Be careful for nothing\" in the older English means \"do not be full of care.\" The Greek is me merimnate: stop being pulled apart. The remedy Paul offers is the opposite of division, a single-minded turning of everything toward God.","walk":"Walk it out: Write the worry down in one sentence. Under it write, \"Lord, I give You this.\" Then write three things God has already done for you. Do it again tonight, and again tomorrow. Anxiety loses ground when it is named, handed over and answered with thanks.","scriptures":[{"ref":"Matthew 6:25-27","text":"Therefore I say unto you, Take no thought for your life, what ye shall eat, or what ye shall drink; nor yet for your body, what ye shall put on. Is not the life more than meat, and the body than raiment? Behold the fowls of the air: for they sow not, neither do they reap, nor gather into barns; yet your heavenly Father feedeth them. Are ye not much better than they? Which of you by taking thought can add one cubit unto his stature?"},{"ref":"Matthew 6:33-34","text":"But seek ye first the kingdom of God, and his righteousness; and all these things shall be added unto you. Take therefore no thought for the morrow: for the morrow shall take thought for the things of itself. Sufficient unto the day is the evil thereof."},{"ref":"Psalm 55:22","text":"Cast thy burden upon the LORD, and he shall sustain thee: he shall never suffer the righteous to be moved."},{"ref":"Isaiah 26:3","text":"Thou wilt keep him in perfect peace, whose mind is stayed on thee: because he trusteth in thee."},{"ref":"1 Peter 5:6-7","text":"Humble yourselves therefore under the mighty hand of God, that he may exalt you in due time: Casting all your care upon him; for he careth for you."}],"prayers":[{"title":"Father, calm my divided mind","text":"Father in heaven, You see the thoughts that run through my mind tonight. I come to You because I cannot hold them together by myself. I confess that I have tried to carry what You never asked me to carry, and that I have trusted my own planning more than Your promises.\n\nLord, I cast every care on You right now, one by one. I name them before You: my money, my family, my health, my future, the things I cannot control. I throw them on You, because You care for me. I take them back no more.\n\nThank You that You are my Father, that You feed the birds and clothe the grass, and that I am worth more to You than they are. Thank You for every time You have carried me before. Let Your peace, which passes all understanding, stand guard over my heart and my mind in Christ Jesus. Teach me to seek first Your kingdom, and trust that You will add what I need. Today is enough. Tomorrow belongs to You. In Jesus' name, Amen."},{"title":"When the storm is loud","text":"Lord Jesus, You slept in the boat while the waves beat over it, and when Your disciples woke You, You spoke and there was a great calm. There is a storm in my heart. Speak to it now. Say to my fear, \"Peace, be still.\"\n\nI refuse to let fear have the last word. I choose to trust the One who rules the winds and the sea. Hold me. Hold my family. Put a guard around my thoughts, and bring to my mind everything true, honorable, right and pure. Where I cannot see the way, be the way. Thank You that You are with me in the boat. Amen."}],"declare":["I will not be anxious about anything. I bring everything to God in prayer with thanksgiving.","The peace of God guards my heart and my mind in Christ Jesus.","My Father knows what I need before I ask.","I cast every care on the Lord, for He cares for me."]},"fear":{"doc":["Fear is the first emotion recorded after the fall: \"I was afraid... and I hid\" (Genesis 3:10). From that moment, fear and hiding have gone together. The Bible's answer to fear is not to be fearless in ourselves but to know who is with us. The most repeated command in Scripture, in one form or another, is \"Fear not,\" and it is almost always followed by \"for I am with thee.\" Courage in the Bible is never a personal trait; it is the fruit of presence.","Isaiah 41:10 stacks the promises: \"I am with thee... I am thy God... I will strengthen thee... I will help thee... I will uphold thee with the right hand of my righteousness.\" The right hand in Scripture is the hand of power and covenant. God does not say the water will not come; He says that when you pass through the waters, He will be with you (Isaiah 43:2). The doctrine is that of the covenant presence of God: He binds Himself to His people and walks into the fire with them, as the fourth figure in Nebuchadnezzar's furnace (Daniel 3:25).","Paul writes to Timothy, who was timid: \"God hath not given us the spirit of fear; but of power, and of love, and of a sound mind\" (2 Timothy 1:7). The word for fear here is deilia, cowardice, a shrinking back. It does not come from God. What He gives instead has three parts: power to act, love to give, and a sound mind, a self-controlled, steady way of thinking. Fear shrinks the mind; the Spirit steadies it.","John goes deepest: \"There is no fear in love; but perfect love casteth out fear: because fear hath torment\" (1 John 4:18). The cure for fear is to be convinced of being loved. The more you know the Father's love, the less room there is for dread. And there is a holy fear, the fear of the Lord, which is reverence and which actually frees us from every other fear (Proverbs 14:26)."],"christ":"Revealed in Christ: At the lowest point of fear, Jesus said to a frightened man, \"Fear not; I am the first and the last: I am he that liveth, and was dead; and, behold, I am alive for evermore\" (Revelation 1:17-18). The risen Christ holds the keys of death and the grave. Whatever you fear ends at a door He has the key to.","word":"The word behind the word: The Hebrew al tira, \"do not fear,\" is a command in the form of a gift. It is often spoken together with \"I am with you\" in the same breath. The comfort is never \"nothing bad will happen\" but \"you will not face it alone.\"","walk":"Walk it out: Write down the thing you fear most. Beside it write, \"God is with me in this.\" Read Isaiah 41:10 aloud three times, putting your own name in it. Then take one small step you have been avoiding. Courage grows by doing the next right thing in His presence.","scriptures":[{"ref":"Isaiah 41:10","text":"Fear thou not; for I am with thee: be not dismayed; for I am thy God: I will strengthen thee; yea, I will help thee; yea, I will uphold thee with the right hand of my righteousness."},{"ref":"Isaiah 43:1-2","text":"But now thus saith the LORD that created thee, O Jacob, and he that formed thee, O Israel, Fear not: for I have redeemed thee, I have called thee by thy name; thou art mine. When thou passest through the waters, I will be with thee; and through the rivers, they shall not overflow thee: when thou walkest through the fire, thou shalt not be burned; neither shall the flame kindle upon thee."},{"ref":"Psalm 23:4","text":"Yea, though I walk through the valley of the shadow of death, I will fear no evil: for thou art with me; thy rod and thy staff they comfort me."},{"ref":"Psalm 27:1","text":"A Psalm of David. The LORD is my light and my salvation; whom shall I fear? the LORD is the strength of my life; of whom shall I be afraid?"},{"ref":"Joshua 1:9","text":"Have not I commanded thee? Be strong and of a good courage; be not afraid, neither be thou dismayed: for the LORD thy God is with thee whithersoever thou goest."},{"ref":"2 Timothy 1:7","text":"For God hath not given us the spirit of fear; but of power, and of love, and of a sound mind."}],"prayers":[{"title":"Lord, You are with me","text":"Father, You are my light and my salvation; whom shall I fear? You are the strength of my life; of whom shall I be afraid? I bring my fear to You. I name it: (say it aloud). It feels bigger than I am. But You are bigger than it.\n\nLord, You said, \"Fear not, for I am with thee; be not dismayed, for I am thy God.\" I take You at Your word. Strengthen me, help me, uphold me with Your righteous right hand. Where I have been hiding, call me out, as You called Adam: \"Where art thou?\" I come out of hiding. I come to You.\n\nFill me with Your perfect love, which casts out fear. Give me power to act, love to give and a sound mind to think. Let me walk through the valley of the shadow of death and fear no evil, for You are with me; Your rod and Your staff comfort me. In Jesus' name, Amen."},{"title":"Courage for today","text":"Lord, be strong and courageous in me today. I do not feel brave, but I choose to obey. Courage is not the absence of fear, it is taking the next step while You hold my hand, and so I take it.\n\nGo before me into every room, every conversation, every decision. Be on my right hand and my left, and behind me. Be my shield, my fortress and my very present help in trouble. Where I have to speak, give me words. Where I have to wait, give me peace. Where I have to walk away, give me strength.\n\nThank You that You have not given me the spirit of fear, but of power, love and a sound mind. Thank You that You hold my right hand and say to me, Fear not, I will help thee. I am not alone, and I will not be put to shame. In Jesus' name, Amen."}],"declare":["The Lord is my light and my salvation; whom shall I fear?","God has not given me a spirit of fear, but of power, love and a sound mind.","Perfect love casts out fear, and I am loved by God.","I will be strong and courageous, for the Lord my God is with me wherever I go."]},"grief":{"doc":["Grief is love with nowhere to go, and the Bible gives it room. The Psalms contain more laments than praises. Job sat with his friends for seven days in silence. Jesus, who knew He would raise Lazarus in minutes, still stood at the tomb and wept (John 11:35). The shortest verse in the Bible is the longest comfort for the grieving: the Son of God is not embarrassed by tears.","The doctrine here is the compassion of God and the hope of the resurrection. \"The Lord is nigh unto them that are of a broken heart; and saveth such as be of a contrite spirit\" (Psalm 34:18). The Hebrew for nigh means near, within reach. He does not wait for you to finish grieving before drawing close. And Paul writes that we do not sorrow \"as others which have no hope\" (1 Thessalonians 4:13). Christians grieve fully, but with hope: for those who die in Christ, death is a sleep before the morning of the resurrection, and we will be reunited with them.","Jesus promised, \"Blessed are they that mourn: for they shall be comforted\" (Matthew 5:4). The word for comfort comes from parakaleo, to call alongside. It is the root of the title Paraclete, given to the Holy Spirit. God's comfort is a Person who comes alongside, not a slogan. Paul calls Him \"the God of all comfort, who comforteth us in all our tribulation, that we may be able to comfort them which are in any trouble\" (2 Corinthians 1:3-4). Your grief, held in His hand, will one day make you a comforter to someone else.","At the end of the story, \"God shall wipe away all tears from their eyes; and there shall be no more death, neither sorrow, nor crying, neither shall there be any more pain\" (Revelation 21:4). Notice that God Himself does the wiping. The tears are real, and the hand that wipes them is real too."],"christ":"Revealed in Christ: Isaiah calls Him \"a man of sorrows, and acquainted with grief\" (Isaiah 53:3). Jesus did not only watch human sorrow; He entered it and carried it to the cross, and then He rose. Because He rose, every grave in Christ is a temporary address.","word":"The word behind the word: The Hebrew word often translated \"comfort,\" nacham, also means to sigh, to breathe deeply, to be sorry together with someone. God's comfort begins with sitting beside you and sighing with you.","walk":"Walk it out: Do not rush your grief. Write a letter to the one you lost, or say their name aloud and thank God for one specific gift they gave you. Reach out to one person today and tell them honestly how you are. Grief shared is grief halved.","scriptures":[{"ref":"Psalm 34:18","text":"The LORD is nigh unto them that are of a broken heart; and saveth such as be of a contrite spirit."},{"ref":"Psalm 147:3","text":"He healeth the broken in heart, and bindeth up their wounds."},{"ref":"Matthew 5:4","text":"Blessed are they that mourn: for they shall be comforted."},{"ref":"John 11:25-26","text":"Jesus said unto her, I am the resurrection, and the life: he that believeth in me, though he were dead, yet shall he live: And whosoever liveth and believeth in me shall never die. Believest thou this?"},{"ref":"1 Thessalonians 4:13-14","text":"But I would not have you to be ignorant, brethren, concerning them which are asleep, that ye sorrow not, even as others which have no hope. For if we believe that Jesus died and rose again, even so them also which sleep in Jesus will God bring with him."},{"ref":"Revelation 21:4","text":"And God shall wipe away all tears from their eyes; and there shall be no more death, neither sorrow, nor crying, neither shall there be any more pain: for the former things are passed away."}],"prayers":[{"title":"In the valley of loss","text":"Father of mercies and God of all comfort, my heart is heavy and the pain is real. I miss them so much. I thank You that You are near to the brokenhearted, and that You do not despise my tears. You keep count of my sorrows and put my tears in Your bottle.\n\nLord Jesus, You wept at Lazarus's grave, and so I know that You understand. Weep with me now. Hold me when I cannot hold myself. Let me cry without shame and rest without guilt.\n\nThank You for the gift of the one I lost, and for every memory. Thank You for the hope of the resurrection: that You are the resurrection and the life, and that those who die in You will live. Give me strength for today only. Surround me with people who love me. Holy Spirit, my Comforter, come alongside me. Heal my broken heart and bind up my wounds. One day You will wipe away every tear. Until then, carry me. In Jesus' name, Amen."},{"title":"When the pain comes back","text":"Lord, the grief came back like a wave tonight. It caught me when I was not ready, and I am tired of being strong. I do not need answers; I need You. Be near.\n\nYou collect my tears in Your bottle and You count them. Nothing I am feeling is a surprise to You or a failure before You. Thank You that Jesus wept at a grave, so I may weep too without shame. Remind me that this ache is love with nowhere to go, and that love is never wasted. Hold the memories that hurt and the ones that heal.\n\nI believe that You are making all things new, and that You will wipe away every tear. Until that day, carry me. Let Your peace settle on my heart, give me sleep tonight, and bring me gently to the morning. Send a kind person to sit with me when I need it. In Jesus' name, Amen."}],"declare":["The Lord is near to the brokenhearted, and He saves the crushed in spirit.","I do not grieve as those who have no hope.","Jesus is the resurrection and the life, and in Him I will live.","God will wipe away every tear from my eyes."]},"depression":{"doc":["Depression is not a sign of weak faith. Some of the Bible's greatest people wrote and prayed from the pit: David, Elijah under the juniper tree wanting to die (1 Kings 19:4), Jeremiah, Job, and the sons of Korah in Psalm 42. The Bible treats the downcast soul with tenderness and honesty, and it never tells the sufferer to snap out of it. It models a conversation with your own soul.","In Psalm 42:5 the writer talks to himself: \"Why art thou cast down, O my soul? and why art thou disquieted in me? hope thou in God: for I shall yet praise him.\" The words cast down come from a Hebrew verb meaning bowed low. The psalmist does not pretend the feeling is absent. He questions it, and then he speaks to it, directing his soul toward God. Hope in Hebrew, yachal, means to wait, to expect with patience. Hope is not a mood; it is an act of waiting on a faithful God.","The doctrine is God's tender care for the weak. When Elijah despaired, God did not rebuke him; He sent an angel with food and let him sleep (1 Kings 19:5-8), and then spoke to him in a still small voice. God deals with the whole person, body and soul. That is why it is wise, and not unfaithful, to seek rest, food, daylight, friends and medical help alongside prayer.","Isaiah 42:3 says of the Messiah: \"A bruised reed shall he not break, and the smoking flax shall he not quench.\" You may feel like a bent reed or a smoldering wick. He does not snap you or snuff you out. And Isaiah 61:3 promises \"the garment of praise for the spirit of heaviness.\" Praise is not denial of pain; it is a garment we put on when we cannot feel anything else."],"christ":"Revealed in Christ: Jesus said, \"My soul is exceeding sorrowful, even unto death\" (Matthew 26:38). He knows the weight on the soul. And He brought light into the darkest day: the cross, and then the third morning. No darkness is final in Him.","word":"The word behind the word: The Hebrew nefesh, translated \"soul,\" means the whole living self, your breath, your appetite, your life. When the psalmist speaks to his soul, he speaks to his whole self and calls it back to God.","walk":"Walk it out: Do one small thing today: open a window, take a short walk, call one person, eat a proper meal. Then pray one honest sentence, even \"God, I am here.\" If the heaviness lasts for weeks, please see a doctor or counselor; asking for help is not a lack of faith.","scriptures":[{"ref":"Psalm 42:5","text":"Why art thou cast down, O my soul? and why art thou disquieted in me? hope thou in God: for I shall yet praise him for the help of his countenance."},{"ref":"Psalm 42:11","text":"Why art thou cast down, O my soul? and why art thou disquieted within me? hope thou in God: for I shall yet praise him, who is the health of my countenance, and my God."},{"ref":"Psalm 40:1-2","text":"To the chief Musician, A Psalm of David. I waited patiently for the LORD; and he inclined unto me, and heard my cry. He brought me up also out of an horrible pit, out of the miry clay, and set my feet upon a rock, and established my goings."},{"ref":"Isaiah 42:3","text":"A bruised reed shall he not break, and the smoking flax shall he not quench: he shall bring forth judgment unto truth."},{"ref":"Isaiah 61:3","text":"To appoint unto them that mourn in Zion, to give unto them beauty for ashes, the oil of joy for mourning, the garment of praise for the spirit of heaviness; that they might be called trees of righteousness, the planting of the LORD, that he might be glorified."},{"ref":"Lamentations 3:21-23","text":"This I recall to my mind, therefore have I hope. It is of the LORD'S mercies that we are not consumed, because his compassions fail not. They are new every morning: great is thy faithfulness."}],"prayers":[{"title":"When my soul is cast down","text":"Lord, my soul is bowed low and I do not have the words. I come to You as I am. You know the heaviness I carry. You do not condemn me for it. You are near to the brokenhearted and You save those who are crushed in spirit.\n\nI speak to my own soul now, as the psalmist did: Why are you cast down, O my soul? Hope in God, for I shall yet praise Him. Even if I cannot feel it, I choose to wait on You. Lift me out of the pit, set my feet on a rock and put a new song in my mouth.\n\nSend me what I need: rest, light, kind people, wise help. Give me courage to ask for it, from friends, from my pastor and from a doctor if I need one. Give me the garment of praise in place of the spirit of heaviness. Thank You that You will not break a bruised reed or quench a smoking wick. Hold me through this night, and bring me to the morning. In Jesus' name, Amen."},{"title":"A small prayer for a hard day","text":"God, I do not have strength to pray long, so I will just come. Be with me in this heaviness. You said You are near to the brokenhearted and save those of a contrite spirit. I am here, and I need You near.\n\nDo for me what I cannot do for myself. Lift the weight a little. Help me get up, wash, eat something, open the curtains, send one message to one safe person. Help me take the next small step, and the one after, and let that be enough for today.\n\nSilence the voice that says I am a burden and that nothing will change. Speak truth over it: that I am loved, that this season is not forever, that You are with me in the pit. Send the right people, and the right help. If I am in danger, give me courage to tell someone and to call for help at once. Thank You that Your mercies are new every morning, and that great is Your faithfulness. In Jesus' name, Amen."}],"declare":["The Lord is near to me, and He will not leave me in the pit.","I will hope in God, for I shall yet praise Him.","He will not break the bruised reed or quench the smoking flax.","His mercies are new every morning."]},"loneliness":{"doc":["The first thing God called \"not good\" in all creation was loneliness: \"It is not good that the man should be alone\" (Genesis 2:18). We were made for fellowship, with God and with each other. Loneliness is a real pain, and the Bible does not shame it. But it also reveals the deepest answer: you are never actually alone, because God is with you.","The doctrine is the omnipresence and the covenant faithfulness of God. David asks, \"Whither shall I go from thy spirit? or whither shall I flee from thy presence?\" (Psalm 139:7), and answers that even in the uttermost parts of the sea, God's hand leads him. And Hebrews 13:5 quotes the promise with a force that English hides: in the Greek there are five negatives piled up, something like \"I will never, never, no never leave you, no never forsake you.\" It is among the strongest promises in the New Testament.","God also sets the lonely in families: \"God setteth the solitary in families\" (Psalm 68:6). The church is meant to be that family, a place where the fatherless, widows and strangers belong. Loneliness is often answered by God through people, so we are called both to receive and to become that answer for others.","Even Jesus knew loneliness. On the night He was betrayed, His friends fell asleep, and at the cross He cried, \"My God, my God, why hast thou forsaken me?\" (Matthew 27:46). He was forsaken so that you would never be. And after His resurrection His last words to His disciples were, \"Lo, I am with you alway, even unto the end of the world\" (Matthew 28:20)."],"christ":"Revealed in Christ: His very name is Emmanuel, \"God with us\" (Matthew 1:23). The whole Gospel is the story of God refusing to stay away. He came to where you are.","word":"The word behind the word: In the promise \"I will never leave thee,\" the Hebrew raphah means to let go, to drop, to slacken the grip. God says, \"I will never loosen My hold on you.\"","walk":"Walk it out: Today, reach out to one person: send a message, make a short call, or visit someone. Pray: \"Lord, show me who needs a friend.\" Join one gathering of believers this week, even if you only sit at the back. Connection often starts with one small step toward someone else.","scriptures":[{"ref":"Genesis 2:18","text":"And the LORD God said, It is not good that the man should be alone; I will make him an help meet for him."},{"ref":"Psalm 68:6","text":"God setteth the solitary in families: he bringeth out those which are bound with chains: but the rebellious dwell in a dry land."},{"ref":"Psalm 139:7-10","text":"Whither shall I go from thy spirit? or whither shall I flee from thy presence? If I ascend up into heaven, thou art there: if I make my bed in hell, behold, thou art there. If I take the wings of the morning, and dwell in the uttermost parts of the sea; Even there shall thy hand lead me, and thy right hand shall hold me."},{"ref":"Deuteronomy 31:8","text":"And the LORD, he it is that doth go before thee; he will be with thee, he will not fail thee, neither forsake thee: fear not, neither be dismayed."},{"ref":"Hebrews 13:5","text":"Let your conversation be without covetousness; and be content with such things as ye have: for he hath said, I will never leave thee, nor forsake thee."},{"ref":"Matthew 28:20","text":"Teaching them to observe all things whatsoever I have commanded you: and, lo, I am with you alway, even unto the end of the world. Amen."}],"prayers":[{"title":"You are with me","text":"Father, I feel alone tonight. The house is quiet and the silence is loud. But Your Word says You are near, and I take You at Your Word. You will never leave me nor forsake me. You are closer than my own breath. Come into this room and let me feel Your presence.\n\nForgive me where loneliness has made me bitter, or made me hide. Heal the hurts that make it hard to trust. Lord, You set the solitary in families. Place me in a family of believers who will welcome me, and teach me to welcome others. Send me a friend. Make me a friend.\n\nI thank You that Jesus was forsaken so that I will never be. Let that truth sink from my head to my heart. Fill the empty space in me with Your love. Lead me into the day with a quiet courage. In Jesus' name, Amen."},{"title":"For the lonely around me","text":"Lord, open my eyes to the lonely people near me: the widow, the stranger, the one who sits alone at the back, the neighbour whose door never opens. You set the solitary in families, and You are the Father of the fatherless. Use me to be part of how You do it.\n\nTake me out of my own heaviness and my own shyness. Give me the courage to greet, to call, to visit, to invite someone to a meal. Give me the right word and the patience to listen without hurrying. Let no one leave my company feeling smaller than they came.\n\nWhere I am lonely myself, meet me, and lead me to people who will walk with me. Make our homes and our churches places where no one is forgotten. Use me to be Your hands and Your voice today, and let someone know through me that they are seen, loved and not forgotten. In Jesus' name, Amen."}],"declare":["The Lord will never leave me nor forsake me.","God is with me always, to the end of the age.","I am a member of God's family, and I belong.","In His presence there is fullness of joy."]},"anger":{"doc":["Anger itself is not a sin. God is often described as angry at injustice and evil, and Jesus was angry in the temple. Paul commands, \"Be ye angry, and sin not: let not the sun go down upon your wrath\" (Ephesians 4:26). The word he uses for the second kind of anger, parorgismos, means a simmering, provoked state, anger that is nursed. The command is to feel it honestly, and then to deal with it quickly before it turns into bitterness.","The doctrine of the human heart is clear: James says that \"the wrath of man worketh not the righteousness of God\" (James 1:20). Our anger usually defends our own comfort, pride or rights, not God's righteousness. Proverbs adds that \"a soft answer turneth away wrath\" (Proverbs 15:1), and that the one who rules his spirit is better than one who takes a city (Proverbs 16:32). Self-control is a form of strength, and the Holy Spirit grows it in us.","Under much anger there is hurt, fear or injustice. God wants us to bring all three to Him. Romans 12:19 says, \"Vengeance is mine; I will repay, saith the Lord.\" Handing a wrong over to God is not weakness. It is trusting that the Judge of all the earth will do right (Genesis 18:25), and it frees us from carrying the weight of revenge.","Forgiveness finishes what self-control starts. \"Be ye kind one to another, tenderhearted, forgiving one another, even as God for Christ's sake hath forgiven you\" (Ephesians 4:32). The anger that is surrendered becomes mercy, and that is a work that only the Spirit can do."],"christ":"Revealed in Christ: \"When he was reviled, reviled not again; when he suffered, he threatened not; but committed himself to him that judgeth righteously\" (1 Peter 2:23). Jesus shows us the way of handing anger over to the Father.","word":"The word behind the word: \"Slow to anger\" in Hebrew is erek appayim, literally \"long of nostrils.\" The picture is of a person who breathes slowly while the fire rises. It is the exact description God gives of Himself (Exodus 34:6).","walk":"Walk it out: When you feel anger rising, stop, breathe slowly and pray one sentence: \"Lord, give me Your heart for this.\" Before bed, name what you are angry about and hand it to Him. If you need to, speak to the person privately and kindly. Do not let the sun set on it.","scriptures":[{"ref":"Ephesians 4:26-27","text":"Be ye angry, and sin not: let not the sun go down upon your wrath: Neither give place to the devil."},{"ref":"Ephesians 4:31-32","text":"Let all bitterness, and wrath, and anger, and clamour, and evil speaking, be put away from you, with all malice: And be ye kind one to another, tenderhearted, forgiving one another, even as God for Christ's sake hath forgiven you."},{"ref":"James 1:19-20","text":"Wherefore, my beloved brethren, let every man be swift to hear, slow to speak, slow to wrath: For the wrath of man worketh not the righteousness of God."},{"ref":"Proverbs 15:1","text":"A soft answer turneth away wrath: but grievous words stir up anger."},{"ref":"Proverbs 16:32","text":"He that is slow to anger is better than the mighty; and he that ruleth his spirit than he that taketh a city."},{"ref":"Romans 12:19","text":"Dearly beloved, avenge not yourselves, but rather give place unto wrath: for it is written, Vengeance is mine; I will repay, saith the Lord."}],"prayers":[{"title":"Lord, take my anger","text":"Father, I am angry, and You know exactly why. I do not hide it from You. Thank You that You are slow to anger and rich in mercy. Forgive me for the angry words and thoughts that have come from my heart, and for the times I have let the sun go down on my wrath.\n\nI hand over to You the one who hurt me. I hand over my right to get even. You are the righteous Judge, and You will do right. Heal the wound underneath my anger: the hurt, the fear, the sense of being unseen.\n\nGive me a soft answer. Make me quick to listen, slow to speak and slow to become angry. Fill me with Your Spirit of self-control. Teach me to forgive as You have forgiven me. Where I need to make peace, show me the first step. Soften my heart, and let Your peace rule in it. In Jesus' name, Amen."},{"title":"In the heat of the moment","text":"Lord, I am about to lose my temper. Stop me. Put a guard on my lips and keep watch at the door of my mouth. Let me breathe once, and breathe Your name. Slow my heart. Slow my words.\n\nShow me what is under the anger: hurt, fear, tiredness, pride, a need that was not met. I give You the thing under the thing. Give me a soft answer, since a soft answer turns away wrath. Let me be swift to hear, slow to speak and slow to wrath, for the anger of man does not work the righteousness of God.\n\nIf I have already spoken harshly, give me the humility to go back and say sorry. Let this moment be a place where Your peace wins, and where the person in front of me sees something of Jesus. Cleanse me and cool me. In Jesus' name, Amen."}],"declare":["I am slow to anger and quick to listen.","The Spirit of God gives me self-control.","I leave vengeance to the Lord, and I choose forgiveness.","A soft answer turns away wrath."]},"forgiveness":{"doc":["Forgiveness is at the center of the Gospel. The Greek word aphiemi, to forgive, means to send away, to release. In Christ our sins are sent away as far as the east is from the west (Psalm 103:12). Forgiveness is not forgetting, and it is not pretending that wrong did not happen. It is canceling a debt: you decide that the one who owes you will not be made to pay.","The doctrine is grace. Paul writes, \"forgiving one another, if any man have a quarrel against any: even as Christ forgave you, so also do ye\" (Colossians 3:13). The word forgiving here is charizomai, from charis, grace. To forgive is to give graciously. We forgive because we have been forgiven: the measure of our mercy is not the size of the offense but the size of the debt we were released from. In Jesus' parable of the unforgiving servant (Matthew 18:21-35), the servant who was forgiven ten thousand talents refused to forgive a hundred pence. The point is that we have been forgiven an unpayable debt.","Jesus ties receiving and giving closely together: \"If ye forgive men their trespasses, your heavenly Father will also forgive you\" (Matthew 6:14). This does not mean we earn forgiveness by forgiving. It means that a heart that clings to bitterness has not really understood grace. Forgiveness is also a decision before it is a feeling: you choose it, and the feelings follow in time.","Forgiving does not always mean reconciling. Reconciliation needs repentance and trust, which take two people. Forgiveness takes one. And it does not mean staying in danger: you can forgive an abuser and still protect yourself and others. You hand the debt over to God, who judges justly."],"christ":"Revealed in Christ: From the cross He prayed, \"Father, forgive them; for they know not what they do\" (Luke 23:34). He forgave while the nails were still in. The cross is the place where justice and mercy meet.","word":"The word behind the word: In Hebrew, the word for forgive, salach, is used only of God. Only He truly cancels sin. When we forgive one another, we reflect His image.","walk":"Walk it out: Write the name of the person you need to forgive. Say aloud, \"I release you from what you owe me, and I give the matter to God.\" Pray for their good, even if it is short and hard. Repeat it every time the memory returns, until the sting fades.","scriptures":[{"ref":"Colossians 3:13","text":"Forbearing one another, and forgiving one another, if any man have a quarrel against any: even as Christ forgave you, so also do ye."},{"ref":"Ephesians 4:32","text":"And be ye kind one to another, tenderhearted, forgiving one another, even as God for Christ's sake hath forgiven you."},{"ref":"Matthew 6:14-15","text":"For if ye forgive men their trespasses, your heavenly Father will also forgive you: But if ye forgive not men their trespasses, neither will your Father forgive your trespasses."},{"ref":"Matthew 18:21-22","text":"Then came Peter to him, and said, Lord, how oft shall my brother sin against me, and I forgive him? till seven times? Jesus saith unto him, I say not unto thee, Until seven times: but, Until seventy times seven."},{"ref":"Psalm 103:10-12","text":"He hath not dealt with us after our sins; nor rewarded us according to our iniquities. For as the heaven is high above the earth, so great is his mercy toward them that fear him. As far as the east is from the west, so far hath he removed our transgressions from us."},{"ref":"Luke 23:34","text":"Then said Jesus, Father, forgive them; for they know not what they do. And they parted his raiment, and cast lots."}],"prayers":[{"title":"To forgive from the heart","text":"Father, thank You that You have forgiven me everything in Christ. I could never pay the debt, and You paid it for me. Now I choose to forgive (name the person). They hurt me. What they did was wrong, and it cost me. But I cancel the debt. I release them to You, and I trust You to judge justly.\n\nI ask You to heal the places where I was wounded. Take away bitterness, and keep it from taking root in me. Where I feel I cannot forgive, I ask for Your help, because I cannot do this in my own strength. Bless them. Do them good. Let me see them as You see them.\n\nIf I have wronged someone, show me, and give me courage to ask for forgiveness. Wash me clean and free me from guilt. Let my life show the grace that has been shown to me. In Jesus' name, Amen."},{"title":"Lord, forgive me","text":"Father, I have sinned against You and against others. I do not excuse it, blame anyone, or make it smaller than it is. I name it before You, because You already see it, and You are faithful and just to forgive.\n\nHave mercy on me according to Your steadfast love. Wash me thoroughly from my iniquity and cleanse me from my sin. Create in me a clean heart and renew a right spirit within me. Restore to me the joy of Your salvation and uphold me with a willing spirit. Where my sin harmed someone, show me what to say and what to repair, and give me the courage to do it.\n\nThank You that where sin abounded, grace abounded much more. Thank You that You cast my sins into the depths of the sea and remember them no more. Let me not carry what You have already carried. Teach me to walk in the freedom of the forgiven, and to forgive others as I have been forgiven. In Jesus' name, Amen."}],"declare":["I am forgiven, because Jesus paid for my sin.","I forgive as I have been forgiven.","Bitterness has no place in my heart.","God's grace in me is greater than any wrong done to me."]},"guilt":{"doc":["Guilt has two faces. There is true guilt, the conviction of the Holy Spirit that we have done wrong, which is a gift because it leads us to repent. And there is false guilt and shame, the whispering accusation that says, \"You are beyond forgiveness,\" which is a lie. The Gospel answers both: for true guilt, a full pardon; for false guilt, a loud \"No condemnation!\"","Paul's triumphant statement is Romans 8:1: \"There is therefore now no condemnation to them which are in Christ Jesus.\" The Greek begins with ouden, absolutely none, and the word condemnation, katakrima, is a court word: the sentence of a judge. It is gone. The believer has been tried, the verdict has been delivered and the penalty has been paid by Christ, who \"hath made him to be sin for us, who knew no sin; that we might be made the righteousness of God in him\" (2 Corinthians 5:21). That is called justification, being declared righteous on Christ's account, not on ours.","True conviction works differently from condemnation. Conviction is specific (\"this thing\"), it leads to confession and cleansing (1 John 1:9), and it ends with relief. Condemnation is vague (\"you are bad\"), it makes you hide, and it ends with despair. The Spirit convicts to draw you home; the accuser condemns to push you away. \"The accuser of our brethren is cast down\" (Revelation 12:10), and he has been overcome \"by the blood of the Lamb.\"","God does more than forgive; He forgets in the sense that He will not hold it against you: \"their sins and iniquities will I remember no more\" (Hebrews 8:12). When you confess, the case is closed. You may remember your sin; He does not count it."],"christ":"Revealed in Christ: At the cross, Jesus took the legal charges against us, \"blotting out the handwriting of ordinances that was against us... nailing it to his cross\" (Colossians 2:14). Every charge, every accusation, every record of debt is pinned to a cross, and the cross is empty because He is risen.","word":"The word behind the word: The Hebrew for atonement, kaphar, means to cover, to wipe away. The blood of the sacrifice did not just hide sin; it cleansed. In Christ the covering is complete.","walk":"Walk it out: Take a piece of paper. Write what you feel guilty about, and under it write 1 John 1:9 in your own words. Confess it plainly. Then tear the paper up. When the accusation returns, answer it with Romans 8:1 aloud.","scriptures":[{"ref":"Romans 8:1","text":"There is therefore now no condemnation to them which are in Christ Jesus, who walk not after the flesh, but after the Spirit."},{"ref":"1 John 1:9","text":"If we confess our sins, he is faithful and just to forgive us our sins, and to cleanse us from all unrighteousness."},{"ref":"Psalm 103:12","text":"As far as the east is from the west, so far hath he removed our transgressions from us."},{"ref":"Isaiah 1:18","text":"Come now, and let us reason together, saith the LORD: though your sins be as scarlet, they shall be as white as snow; though they be red like crimson, they shall be as wool."},{"ref":"Hebrews 10:22","text":"Let us draw near with a true heart in full assurance of faith, having our hearts sprinkled from an evil conscience, and our bodies washed with pure water."},{"ref":"Colossians 2:13-14","text":"And you, being dead in your sins and the uncircumcision of your flesh, hath he quickened together with him, having forgiven you all trespasses; Blotting out the handwriting of ordinances that was against us, which was contrary to us, and took it out of the way, nailing it to his cross;"}],"prayers":[{"title":"Free from condemnation","text":"Father, I come to You carrying guilt. Some of it is real, because I have sinned. Some of it is a heavy shame that does not seem to go away. I bring it all to You. Search me, and show me what is truly mine to confess.\n\nI confess my sin plainly: (say it). I do not make excuses. I thank You that You are faithful and just to forgive me and to cleanse me from all unrighteousness. By the blood of Jesus, I am forgiven.\n\nI reject the accuser's voice. Jesus took my punishment, and there is no condemnation for me. I am not what I did. I am Your child, justified, accepted and loved. Free me from shame and from the habit of punishing myself. Help me to forgive myself as You have forgiven me, and teach me to walk in newness of life. In Jesus' name, Amen."},{"title":"When the accuser speaks","text":"Lord Jesus, the accuser is telling me I am finished, that I have done too much, that God has had enough. I bring those words to the light. You are my advocate with the Father, and You are the propitiation for my sins. If I confess my sin, You are faithful and just to forgive me and to cleanse me from all unrighteousness.\n\nI have confessed. I now plead Your blood over my life, and I stand on Your Word: there is therefore now no condemnation to those who are in Christ Jesus. Who shall lay anything to the charge of God's elect? It is God who justifies. Who is he that condemns? It is Christ who died, and who is risen, and who intercedes for me.\n\nBe silent, accuser, in the name of Jesus. I will not carry what Christ has already carried. Give me a clear conscience and a thankful heart. If there is something real to put right with a person, show me, and I will do it. But I refuse shame that Your cross has already paid for. In Jesus' name, Amen."}],"declare":["There is now no condemnation for me in Christ Jesus.","I am forgiven and cleansed by the blood of Jesus.","God remembers my sins no more.","I am the righteousness of God in Christ."]},"marriage":{"doc":["Marriage is the first institution God made, before government and before the church. In Genesis 2:24 the Lord names three movements: a man shall leave, he shall cleave, and they shall be one flesh. To leave means a new first loyalty. To cleave translates the Hebrew dabaq, which is the word for glue or something stuck fast. It describes a covenant bond, not a mood. Marriage is held together by promise, and feelings are meant to follow the promise, not the other way round.","The doctrine of covenant is the heart of it. Malachi 2:14 calls the wife \"the wife of thy covenant,\" and Proverbs 2:17 speaks of \"the covenant of her God.\" A covenant is a vow made before God that binds you even when it costs you. This is why Scripture treats marriage as a picture of something larger: Paul says it is a great mystery concerning Christ and the church (Ephesians 5:32). A husband and wife are living out, in small, the way Christ keeps faith with His people.","Genesis calls the woman an ezer kenegdo, a helper \"meet for him\" (Genesis 2:18). Ezer is a strong word. It is used of God Himself as the helper of Israel (Psalm 33:20). It never means a lesser person. Kenegdo means one who stands opposite and corresponds, an equal match who sees what the other cannot. Marriage is two whole people who complete what is missing in each other, in equal worth and in different strengths.","Paul's instruction in Ephesians 5 begins with \"submitting yourselves one to another in the fear of God\" (verse 21), and the measure he sets for the husband is the cross: \"love your wives, even as Christ also loved the church, and gave himself for it.\" The pattern is sacrifice, not control. Peter adds that a husband who dishonours his wife finds his own prayers hindered (1 Peter 3:7). A marriage is a place where prayer and daily kindness are tied together."],"christ":"Revealed in Christ: Jesus is called the Bridegroom (Matthew 9:15; John 3:29). He pursued His bride at the cost of His life, and He keeps pursuing her. The vow He keeps with you is the standard and the supply for every vow you keep with your spouse. When you cannot find love in yourself, you can draw it from Him and give it on.","word":"The word behind the word: in Genesis 2:24 the Hebrew for one is echad, the same word used in \"the Lord our God is one Lord\" (Deuteronomy 6:4). It is a unity that holds a plurality. Two remain two persons and become one household, one purpose, one covenant. Oneness is not sameness.","walk":"Walk it out: Tonight, say one specific thing you are thankful for in your spouse and one thing you will do this week to serve them. Pray together, even if it is only one sentence. If you are single, pray for the marriages around you, and ask the Lord to make you a person of faithful words now.","scriptures":[{"ref":"Genesis 2:18","text":"And the LORD God said, It is not good that the man should be alone; I will make him an help meet for him."},{"ref":"Genesis 2:24","text":"Therefore shall a man leave his father and his mother, and shall cleave unto his wife: and they shall be one flesh."},{"ref":"Ecclesiastes 4:9-12","text":"Two are better than one; because they have a good reward for their labour. For if they fall, the one will lift up his fellow: but woe to him that is alone when he falleth; for he hath not another to help him up. Again, if two lie together, then they have heat: but how can one be warm alone? And if one prevail against him, two shall withstand him; and a threefold cord is not quickly broken."},{"ref":"Ephesians 5:25","text":"Husbands, love your wives, even as Christ also loved the church, and gave himself for it;"},{"ref":"Colossians 3:12-14","text":"Put on therefore, as the elect of God, holy and beloved, bowels of mercies, kindness, humbleness of mind, meekness, longsuffering; Forbearing one another, and forgiving one another, if any man have a quarrel against any: even as Christ forgave you, so also do ye. And above all these things put on charity, which is the bond of perfectness."},{"ref":"1 Corinthians 13:4-7","text":"Charity suffereth long, and is kind; charity envieth not; charity vaunteth not itself, is not puffed up, Doth not behave itself unseemly, seeketh not her own, is not easily provoked, thinketh no evil; Rejoiceth not in iniquity, but rejoiceth in the truth; Beareth all things, believeth all things, hopeth all things, endureth all things."}],"prayers":[{"title":"Lord, keep our covenant","text":"Father, You are the God who keeps covenant. You joined us together, and I bring my marriage to You as it is today, with its joys and its wounds. I thank You for the good You have placed in my spouse. Forgive me for every harsh word, every silence used as a weapon, every time I put myself first.\n\nLord, teach me to love as Christ loved: patiently, kindly, without keeping score. Where we have grown apart, bridge the distance. Where trust has been broken, begin to heal it, one honest conversation at a time. Guard our home from the lies of comparison and the pull of temptation. Let our words build and not tear down.\n\nMake us one in purpose and one in prayer. Let our home be a place where Your presence is welcome, and where the people who visit see something of Your faithfulness. Where I am single, Lord, prepare me and wait with me, and let me trust Your timing. In Jesus' name, Amen."},{"title":"Father, heal what hurts between us","text":"Gracious Father, You see the tension in our home. I do not want to win an argument and lose a marriage. Give me the courage to listen first, the humility to say sorry first, and the patience to wait for answers.\n\nWhere pride has built a wall, bring it down. Where we have hurt each other in ways we cannot name, bring Your Spirit to the surface and heal it. Restore the friendship that started us. Renew the kindness and the laughter. Teach us to forgive as You forgave us, completely and without bringing it up again.\n\nLet nothing and no one come between us: not work, not money, not family pressure, not the phone. Keep our eyes on each other and on You, and let the love You poured into us keep running over into our home. In Jesus' name, Amen."}],"declare":["My marriage is a covenant, and God keeps covenant with me.","Love is patient and kind in my home.","I choose forgiveness, honesty and faithfulness.","What God has joined, no one will separate."]},"parenting":{"doc":["Children are, in the words of Psalm 127:3, \"an heritage of the Lord.\" The Hebrew word for heritage speaks of something entrusted, not owned. You are a steward of lives that belong first to God. Parenting is therefore a calling before it is a task, and the first doctrine of parenting is humility: the children are His, and you are given a season to shape them toward Him.","Proverbs 22:6 says, \"Train up a child in the way he should go.\" The Hebrew verb chanak, to train, carries the picture of a midwife rubbing the gums of a newborn with date juice to awaken the desire to suck. Training begins by awakening an appetite. The phrase \"in the way he should go\" is literally \"according to his way\" (al pi darko), which many scholars read as the child's own bent and personality. A wise parent studies the child God has made, and guides that child toward the Lord without crushing who they are.","Deuteronomy 6:6-7 gives the method: God's words in your heart first, then \"thou shalt teach them diligently unto thy children, and shalt talk of them when thou sittest in thine house, and when thou walkest by the way, and when thou liest down, and when thou risest up.\" Faith is taught in the ordinary rhythms: meals, drives, bedtime. It is caught as much as taught. Children read the parent before they read the Bible.","Paul gives the balance in Ephesians 6:4: fathers are not to provoke their children to wrath, but to bring them up \"in the nurture and admonition of the Lord.\" Nurture is the gentle side, admonition the corrective side. Hebrews 12 reminds us that God disciplines because He loves, and that is the model: correction that aims at the child's good, never at the parent's anger. Discipline without love breeds bitterness, and love without discipline breeds ruin."],"christ":"Revealed in Christ: Jesus said, \"Suffer the little children to come unto me, and forbid them not\" (Mark 10:14). He took them in His arms and blessed them. Your child is welcome to Him, and so are you, with every failure you carry. The Father you want to imitate is the Father who ran to the prodigal (Luke 15:20). You cannot be a perfect parent, but you can point to a perfect Father.","word":"The word behind the word: \"nurture\" in Ephesians 6:4 is the Greek paideia, the whole training of a child including instruction and loving correction. \"Admonition\" is nouthesia, literally putting a thought into the mind. Both are acts of patient teaching, not of shouting.","walk":"Walk it out: Choose one moment in the day, such as bedtime or the drive, to pray with your child by name. Tell them one thing you see God doing in them. If you have wronged your child, say sorry plainly. It teaches repentance better than a sermon.","scriptures":[{"ref":"Psalm 127:3-5","text":"Lo, children are an heritage of the LORD: and the fruit of the womb is his reward. As arrows are in the hand of a mighty man; so are children of the youth. Happy is the man that hath his quiver full of them: they shall not be ashamed, but they shall speak with the enemies in the gate."},{"ref":"Proverbs 22:6","text":"Train up a child in the way he should go: and when he is old, he will not depart from it."},{"ref":"Deuteronomy 6:6-7","text":"And these words, which I command thee this day, shall be in thine heart: And thou shalt teach them diligently unto thy children, and shalt talk of them when thou sittest in thine house, and when thou walkest by the way, and when thou liest down, and when thou risest up."},{"ref":"Ephesians 6:4","text":"And, ye fathers, provoke not your children to wrath: but bring them up in the nurture and admonition of the Lord."},{"ref":"Isaiah 54:13","text":"And all thy children shall be taught of the LORD; and great shall be the peace of thy children."},{"ref":"3 John 1:4","text":"I have no greater joy than to hear that my children walk in truth."}],"prayers":[{"title":"Father, my child belongs to You","text":"Father, You gave me this child, and I give this child back to You. I thank You for the life You have entrusted to me. Forgive me where I have been impatient, distracted or harsh. Give me wisdom to know the child You made, and love that listens before it corrects.\n\nLord, protect my children from every harm of body and soul. Guard them from the lies of the world, from harmful friendships, from fear and shame. Draw them to know You early, and let them never have to unlearn Your goodness because of how I lived.\n\nHelp me to teach Your Word in the ordinary hours of our home. Let our house be a place of peace, honesty and laughter. When I do not know what to do, give me Your wisdom, which You give without reproach. When I fail, let Your grace cover my children where my strength falls short. In Jesus' name, Amen."},{"title":"Lord, bring my child home","text":"Father, You know the child I am praying for. You know where they are tonight, in body and in heart. I cannot reach every place they go, but You are already there. I ask that Your Spirit would go after them as the shepherd went after the one sheep.\n\nSoften the hard places. Open their eyes to see Your love. Send people into their life who speak truth and kindness. Break the chains of addiction, anger, fear and wrong company, in the name of Jesus. Let them come to themselves, as the prodigal did, and let them find the way back.\n\nGive me the grace to wait without nagging, to love without condition, and to keep the door open. Heal my own heart of guilt and fear. I stand on Your promise that all my children shall be taught of the Lord, and great shall be their peace. In Jesus' name, Amen."}],"declare":["My children are a heritage from the Lord.","God will teach my children, and great will be their peace.","My home serves the Lord.","I parent with patience, truth and grace."]},"finance":{"doc":["Scripture speaks about money more often than about many doctrines we call central, because money exposes whom we trust. The first truth is ownership: \"The earth is the Lord's, and the fulness thereof\" (Psalm 24:1). You are a steward, not an owner. A steward asks, \"What does the Master want done with what He has put in my hand?\" That one question changes budgeting, saving, spending and giving.","The second truth is that God is a provider. Jehovah-Jireh, \"the Lord will see to it\" (Genesis 22:14), was named on the mountain where Abraham was about to give his most precious thing. Provision is not always early. It often arrives at the point of obedience. Philippians 4:19 says, \"my God shall supply all your need according to his riches in glory by Christ Jesus.\" The Greek verb supply, plerosei, means to fill to the full. Need is met from His riches, not from your account.","Third, the Bible guards the heart. \"The love of money is the root of all evil\" (1 Timothy 6:10), not money itself. Jesus said you cannot serve God and mammon (Matthew 6:24). The danger is not owning, it is being owned. The cure is contentment, and the cure for greed is giving. Generosity breaks money's hold on the heart, which is why Scripture commands it before it is felt.","Fourth, Proverbs is practical: diligence brings profit (Proverbs 10:4), the borrower is servant to the lender (22:7), plans made with counsel succeed (15:22), and a wise person saves while a fool consumes (21:20). Faith and planning are partners. God honours honest work and honest weights. Where debt, loss or lack is heavy, the answer is not shame but a step-by-step plan, wise counsel, and trust that He walks with you through it. This devotion is for encouragement and is not financial advice; speak to a qualified adviser about your situation."],"christ":"Revealed in Christ: \"Though he was rich, yet for your sakes he became poor, that ye through his poverty might be rich\" (2 Corinthians 8:9). The one who owned everything laid his riches down. He knows poverty from the inside, born in a borrowed stable and buried in a borrowed tomb. He is not a stranger to your lack, and He is the true treasure that cannot be taken.","word":"The word behind the word: \"riches\" in Philippians 4:19 is ploutos, wealth in abundance. Paul does not say \"out of\" His riches in the way a rich man gives a coin from a large purse. He says \"according to\" them, meaning the supply is measured by the size of the store.","walk":"Walk it out: Write down your income and your regular costs this week, honestly. Choose one thing to give, one thing to save and one thing to cut. Pray over the list. Ask a trusted person to hold you accountable.","scriptures":[{"ref":"Psalm 24:1","text":"A Psalm of David. The earth is the LORD'S, and the fulness thereof; the world, and they that dwell therein."},{"ref":"Proverbs 3:9-10","text":"Honour the LORD with thy substance, and with the firstfruits of all thine increase: So shall thy barns be filled with plenty, and thy presses shall burst out with new wine."},{"ref":"Proverbs 21:5","text":"The thoughts of the diligent tend only to plenteousness; but of every one that is hasty only to want."},{"ref":"Matthew 6:31-33","text":"Therefore take no thought, saying, What shall we eat? or, What shall we drink? or, Wherewithal shall we be clothed? (For after all these things do the Gentiles seek:) for your heavenly Father knoweth that ye have need of all these things. But seek ye first the kingdom of God, and his righteousness; and all these things shall be added unto you."},{"ref":"Philippians 4:19","text":"But my God shall supply all your need according to his riches in glory by Christ Jesus."},{"ref":"Malachi 3:10","text":"Bring ye all the tithes into the storehouse, that there may be meat in mine house, and prove me now herewith, saith the LORD of hosts, if I will not open you the windows of heaven, and pour you out a blessing, that there shall not be room enough to receive it."},{"ref":"2 Corinthians 9:6-8","text":"But this I say, He which soweth sparingly shall reap also sparingly; and he which soweth bountifully shall reap also bountifully. Every man according as he purposeth in his heart, so let him give; not grudgingly, or of necessity: for God loveth a cheerful giver. And God is able to make all grace abound toward you; that ye, always having all sufficiency in all things, may abound to every good work:"}],"prayers":[{"title":"Lord, You are my provider","text":"Father, You are Jehovah-Jireh, the God who sees my need and provides. I bring my finances to You exactly as they are, with the bills, the debts and the fears. I confess that I have sometimes trusted my income more than You, and sometimes been careless with what You gave me.\n\nTeach me to steward well. Give me wisdom to plan, discipline to save, and a generous heart to give. Open honest doors of work and increase. Close the doors that would lead me into loss or dishonesty. Where I owe, show me the path to pay it off, and where I lack, supply from Your riches in glory.\n\nBreak the grip of greed and the fear of lack. Let me never love money more than You. I choose to seek first Your kingdom, trusting that what I need will be added. Thank You for every meal, every roof, every small mercy I have already received. In Jesus' name, Amen."},{"title":"Father, in a season of lack","text":"Lord, tonight things are tight. I will not pretend otherwise before You. You said that You know what I have need of before I ask. I ask You for provision, for wisdom, for open doors, for work, for the right help at the right time.\n\nGuard my heart from shame and from comparison. Protect my family from desperate decisions. Keep me honest when dishonesty looks easier. Give me favour with the people who can help, and give me the humility to ask when I need to.\n\nI remember Your faithfulness: the oil that did not run dry, the bread that fed a crowd, the ravens that fed Elijah. You have not forgotten me. I believe that You will make a way. Let my lack become a place where I meet You more deeply. In Jesus' name, Amen."}],"declare":["The earth is the Lord's, and I am His faithful steward.","My God supplies all my needs according to His riches in glory.","I am free from the love of money.","I give with joy and I work with diligence."]},"purpose":{"doc":["Purpose is not something you invent. It is something you discover in the One who made you. Ephesians 1:11 says we were \"predestinated according to the purpose of him who worketh all things after the counsel of his own will.\" Before you had any plan, God had one. Your first purpose, the one that underlies every other, is to know Him and glorify Him (Isaiah 43:7; John 17:3).","Jeremiah 29:11 is often quoted: \"I know the thoughts that I think toward you, saith the Lord, thoughts of peace, and not of evil, to give you an expected end.\" The Hebrew for thoughts is machashavot, plans and designs. The Hebrew for expected end is acharit v'tikvah, a future and a hope. Notice the context: these words were written to exiles in Babylon, people whose circumstances looked like the opposite of purpose. God's plan does not wait for ideal conditions.","Ephesians 2:10 tells us, \"we are his workmanship, created in Christ Jesus unto good works, which God hath before ordained that we should walk in them.\" The Greek for workmanship is poiema, from which we get the word poem. You are God's composition, His work of art. And the good works were prepared in advance. Your task is not to build a destiny from nothing but to walk into what He has prepared, one faithful step at a time.","Purpose is lived out in small obedience. Joseph's dreams were followed by years of slavery and prison before they were fulfilled. David was anointed king and then returned to the sheep. God uses unseen faithfulness to prepare for open calling. \"He that is faithful in that which is least is faithful also in much\" (Luke 16:10). Do the next right thing, where you are, with all your heart, as unto the Lord (Colossians 3:23). Purpose is found there."],"christ":"Revealed in Christ: Jesus said, \"I have glorified thee on the earth: I have finished the work which thou gavest me to do\" (John 17:4). He lived with a clear purpose, and He completed it, so that yours would never be lost. In Him your life is not an accident. And your calling is joined to His: to make the Father known.","word":"The word behind the word: \"workmanship\" in Ephesians 2:10 is poiema, the same root as poem. Your life is being written by a Master Author. A single line may not make sense by itself, but the whole is meant to be beautiful.","walk":"Walk it out: Write three answers: What has God put in my hand? Who has He put in my path? What is the next faithful step? Do that step today, however small. Ask one trusted believer what they see God doing in you.","scriptures":[{"ref":"Jeremiah 29:11","text":"For I know the thoughts that I think toward you, saith the LORD, thoughts of peace, and not of evil, to give you an expected end."},{"ref":"Ephesians 2:10","text":"For we are his workmanship, created in Christ Jesus unto good works, which God hath before ordained that we should walk in them."},{"ref":"Romans 8:28","text":"And we know that all things work together for good to them that love God, to them who are the called according to his purpose."},{"ref":"Proverbs 19:21","text":"There are many devices in a man's heart; nevertheless the counsel of the LORD, that shall stand."},{"ref":"Psalm 138:8","text":"The LORD will perfect that which concerneth me: thy mercy, O LORD, endureth for ever: forsake not the works of thine own hands."},{"ref":"Colossians 3:23","text":"And whatsoever ye do, do it heartily, as to the Lord, and not unto men;"},{"ref":"Luke 16:10","text":"He that is faithful in that which is least is faithful also in much: and he that is unjust in the least is unjust also in much."}],"prayers":[{"title":"Lord, show me my purpose","text":"Father, You made me on purpose and for a purpose. I surrender my own plans to You and ask You to show me Yours. Let me not live by drift, by comparison or by fear. Settle my heart on the one thing that matters: to know You and make You known.\n\nOpen the doors You want open, and close the ones that lead away from You. Show me the good works You prepared for me before I was born. Give me the faithfulness to be obedient in small things, to work with all my heart, and to wait for Your timing without losing hope.\n\nWhere I feel I have wasted years, redeem the time. Where I feel I am too small, remind me that You choose the weak. Give me ears to hear, courage to step and humility to serve. Let my life be a poem that brings You glory. In Jesus' name, Amen."},{"title":"Father, while I wait","text":"Lord, I am in a season of waiting, and it feels long. I trust that You are not wasting it. Like Joseph in prison, like Moses in the desert, like David with the sheep, let this season shape me for what You have prepared.\n\nGuard me from bitterness and from envy of those who seem ahead of me. Teach me to be faithful where I am, to do small things with great love. Give me patience in the waiting, and let my character grow deeper than my gifts.\n\nI thank You that You work all things together for good. I choose to believe that what You started in me, You will finish. Direct my steps, establish the work of my hands, and let me recognise Your hand when it comes. In Jesus' name, Amen."}],"declare":["I am God's workmanship, created for good works.","God's plans for me are for peace and a future and a hope.","The Lord will perfect that which concerns me.","I am faithful in little and ready for more."]},"health":{"doc":["The Bible takes the body seriously. God made it and called it very good (Genesis 1:31), the Son of God took one (John 1:14), and the Holy Spirit makes it His temple (1 Corinthians 6:19). So illness is neither unimportant nor a sign of a hidden sin by default. Job's friends were wrong to say it was. Jesus said of the man born blind that it was not sin, but that the works of God should be displayed (John 9:3).","God's name as healer comes from Exodus 15:26: \"I am the Lord that healeth thee,\" Jehovah-Rapha. The Hebrew rapha means to mend, to restore, to stitch together. It is used of bodies, of broken hearts (Psalm 147:3), of land and of nations. God heals in many ways: directly, through doctors and medicine, through rest and care, through the community of believers, and in the final resurrection when every tear is wiped away. Using medicine is not a lack of faith; Luke was a physician and Paul told Timothy to take a little wine for his stomach (1 Timothy 5:23).","James 5:14-15 invites the sick to call for the elders of the church, who pray and anoint with oil, \"and the prayer of faith shall save the sick.\" The Greek sozo means to save, to heal and to make whole. God's concern is the whole person, body, mind, spirit and relationships. Sometimes He heals quickly, sometimes slowly, and sometimes He gives grace to endure. Paul prayed three times that his thorn would be removed, and the answer was, \"My grace is sufficient for thee\" (2 Corinthians 12:9). That is not a failure of prayer. It is a different answer from a Father who sees everything.","Faith does not mean denying symptoms or refusing care. It means bringing them to God while doing what is wise. Take your medicine, see your doctor, rest, eat well, and pray. For long illness, God also heals the heart: He is near to the brokenhearted. If you are caring for someone who is sick, your service is seen by Him. This devotion does not replace medical advice; please see a doctor or nurse for any health concern."],"christ":"Revealed in Christ: Matthew applies Isaiah to Jesus: \"Himself took our infirmities, and bare our sicknesses\" (Matthew 8:17). He healed because He had compassion, and He still has it. He also carries the scars of His own suffering, so He understands pain. Every healing He did was a sign of the world that is coming, where there is no more death, nor sorrow, nor crying, neither shall there be any more pain (Revelation 21:4).","word":"The word behind the word: \"Rapha\" in Exodus 15:26 is related to the word for a physician. God puts Himself in the position of the doctor of His people. The name Jehovah-Rapha was given at Marah, at the bitter water made sweet, which shows that healing begins where we meet bitterness with trust.","walk":"Walk it out: Name the health concern before God plainly. Book the appointment or take the step you have been avoiding. Ask one person to pray with you. Thank Him for one part of your body that is working today.","scriptures":[{"ref":"Exodus 15:26","text":"And said, If thou wilt diligently hearken to the voice of the LORD thy God, and wilt do that which is right in his sight, and wilt give ear to his commandments, and keep all his statutes, I will put none of these diseases upon thee, which I have brought upon the Egyptians: for I am the LORD that healeth thee."},{"ref":"Psalm 103:2-3","text":"Bless the LORD, O my soul, and forget not all his benefits: Who forgiveth all thine iniquities; who healeth all thy diseases;"},{"ref":"Psalm 147:3","text":"He healeth the broken in heart, and bindeth up their wounds."},{"ref":"Isaiah 53:5","text":"But he was wounded for our transgressions, he was bruised for our iniquities: the chastisement of our peace was upon him; and with his stripes we are healed."},{"ref":"James 5:14-15","text":"Is any sick among you? let him call for the elders of the church; and let them pray over him, anointing him with oil in the name of the Lord: And the prayer of faith shall save the sick, and the Lord shall raise him up; and if he have committed sins, they shall be forgiven him."},{"ref":"Jeremiah 30:17","text":"For I will restore health unto thee, and I will heal thee of thy wounds, saith the LORD; because they called thee an Outcast, saying, This is Zion, whom no man seeketh after."},{"ref":"2 Corinthians 12:9","text":"And he said unto me, My grace is sufficient for thee: for my strength is made perfect in weakness. Most gladly therefore will I rather glory in my infirmities, that the power of Christ may rest upon me."}],"prayers":[{"title":"Lord, You are my healer","text":"Father, You are Jehovah-Rapha, the Lord who heals. I bring my body to You, with every pain, every test result and every fear. I thank You that you made me wonderfully. I ask for healing in Jesus' name.\n\nGive wisdom to my doctors and nurses. Guide every treatment and every decision. Where there is infection, inflammation or disease, speak peace. Strengthen what is weak. Give me patience in the waiting rooms and courage in the hard conversations. Keep me from despair, and fill my room with Your peace.\n\nI believe that You are able, and I trust You whether the answer is quick or slow. Let nothing steal my hope, and let this season draw me closer to You. Hold my family and caregivers up. I give You thanks in advance, and I give You all the glory. In Jesus' name, Amen."},{"title":"Father, strengthen the one who is sick","text":"Lord, I stand in the gap for someone I love who is sick. You see them. You know the name, the diagnosis and the fear. I ask for Your healing touch, in whatever way You choose.\n\nBe their comfort in the night. Be their strength in the treatment. Surround them with people who love them well. Where they are afraid, give them peace beyond understanding. Where they are tired, renew their strength like the eagles.\n\nGive me wisdom to serve them, patience when I am weary, and words that bring life. Let this trial deepen our faith and not shrink it. Whatever the outcome, let us know that You are near. In Jesus' name, Amen."}],"declare":["The Lord is my healer.","My body is a temple of the Holy Spirit.","God's grace is sufficient for me.","I trust Him with my health and my future."]},"doubt":{"doc":["Doubt is not the opposite of faith. Unbelief is. Doubt is faith asking a question. The Bible is full of honest doubters: Abraham, Gideon, Thomas, John the Baptist in prison, and a father who said to Jesus, \"Lord, I believe; help thou mine unbelief\" (Mark 9:24). Jesus did not turn any of them away. He answered each one with patience and evidence.","The Greek word for doubt in the New Testament is often distazo, to stand in two places, or dipsychos, double-minded, \"two-souled\" (James 1:8). Doubt is not a lack of intelligence. It is a divided loyalty, one foot on the rock and one on the waves. When Peter walked on the water he did well until he saw the wind and began to sink. Jesus did not say, \"You have no faith.\" He said, \"O thou of little faith, wherefore didst thou doubt?\" (Matthew 14:31). Little faith is still faith, and He caught Peter's hand.","Faith has foundations. John wrote his Gospel \"that ye might believe\" (John 20:31). Luke wrote \"that thou mightest know the certainty\" (Luke 1:4). The resurrection was witnessed by hundreds (1 Corinthians 15:3-8). Creation, conscience and the coherence of Scripture all testify. Honest questions are welcome, and answers exist. The way through doubt is to bring it to God, to the Word and to wise believers, not to hide it.","Some things we will not understand now. \"For now we see through a glass, darkly; but then face to face\" (1 Corinthians 13:12). Faith is not certainty about every detail; it is trust in a person. Thomas wanted to touch the wounds, and Jesus invited him, then said, \"blessed are they that have not seen, and yet have believed\" (John 20:29). Walk with God long enough, and the memories of His faithfulness will outweigh the questions."],"christ":"Revealed in Christ: Jesus meets doubt with nearness. He walked with the two on the road to Emmaus and explained the Scriptures (Luke 24:27). He showed Thomas His hands. He is not threatened by your questions. He is the answer, the one in whom all the treasures of wisdom and knowledge are hidden (Colossians 2:3).","word":"The word behind the word: in James 1:6 the doubter is \"like a wave of the sea driven with the wind and tossed.\" The Greek picture is a sea that has no anchor. Hebrews 6:19 offers the opposite: hope as \"an anchor of the soul, both sure and stedfast.\" Anchor your questions to Christ.","walk":"Walk it out: Write down the honest question. Next to it, write what you do know for sure about God's character. Read John 20 slowly. Then talk to a wise believer, a pastor or a friend. Questions shared lose their power to isolate.","scriptures":[{"ref":"Mark 9:23-24","text":"Jesus said unto him, If thou canst believe, all things are possible to him that believeth. And straightway the father of the child cried out, and said with tears, Lord, I believe; help thou mine unbelief."},{"ref":"Matthew 14:29-31","text":"And he said, Come. And when Peter was come down out of the ship, he walked on the water, to go to Jesus. But when he saw the wind boisterous, he was afraid; and beginning to sink, he cried, saying, Lord, save me. And immediately Jesus stretched forth his hand, and caught him, and said unto him, O thou of little faith, wherefore didst thou doubt?"},{"ref":"John 20:27-29","text":"Then saith he to Thomas, Reach hither thy finger, and behold my hands; and reach hither thy hand, and thrust it into my side: and be not faithless, but believing. And Thomas answered and said unto him, My Lord and my God. Jesus saith unto him, Thomas, because thou hast seen me, thou hast believed: blessed are they that have not seen, and yet have believed."},{"ref":"James 1:5-6","text":"If any of you lack wisdom, let him ask of God, that giveth to all men liberally, and upbraideth not; and it shall be given him. But let him ask in faith, nothing wavering. For he that wavereth is like a wave of the sea driven with the wind and tossed."},{"ref":"Hebrews 11:1","text":"Now faith is the substance of things hoped for, the evidence of things not seen."},{"ref":"Hebrews 6:19","text":"Which hope we have as an anchor of the soul, both sure and stedfast, and which entereth into that within the veil;"},{"ref":"Jude 1:22","text":"And of some have compassion, making a difference:"}],"prayers":[{"title":"Lord, help my unbelief","text":"Father, I come to You as I am, with faith and with questions. I believe, Lord; help my unbelief. I do not want to pretend, and I do not want to drift. Meet me in the place where my faith feels thin.\n\nGive me honest answers, and give me peace where answers do not come. Remind me of the times You have been faithful. Show me again who Jesus is. Guard my mind from the lies that say You are distant or uncaring. Lead me to people who can walk with me, and to Your Word, which is truth.\n\nLike Thomas, I need to know that You are real. Reveal Yourself to me. Anchor my soul, so that the waves do not sink me. Grow my faith until it holds even when I cannot see. In Jesus' name, Amen."},{"title":"Father, steady me","text":"Lord, my mind is restless tonight. Voices say that You are not there, that prayer is empty, that I have been fooled. I bring these voices to You instead of to my own fears.\n\nSpeak louder than the doubt. Let Your Spirit bear witness with my spirit that I am Your child. Bring back to mind the moments You have answered, comforted and carried me. Keep me from being tossed like a wave. Make me single-minded in my love for You.\n\nGive me hunger for Your Word, and humility to ask for help. I thank You that You do not break a bruised reed. I take Your hand as Peter did, and I trust You to hold me. In Jesus' name, Amen."}],"declare":["Lord, I believe; help my unbelief.","Hope is the anchor of my soul.","Faith comes by hearing the Word of God.","He who began a good work in me will complete it."]},"temptation":{"doc":["Temptation is not sin. Jesus was \"in all points tempted like as we are, yet without sin\" (Hebrews 4:15). The moment of testing is not the moment of failure. Sin begins when desire is welcomed and acted on (James 1:14-15). That is the anatomy of a fall: desire, then enticement, then conception, then sin, then death. If you can interrupt it early, you win.","The Greek word peirasmos can mean a test or a temptation, depending on its purpose. God never tempts anyone to sin (James 1:13), but He allows tests that prove and strengthen faith. The same circumstance can be a test from God, to bring out your faith, and a temptation from the enemy, to bring out your failure. The difference is the direction in which you are pulled: toward obedience or away from it.","1 Corinthians 10:13 is a great promise: \"There hath no temptation taken you but such as is common to man: but God is faithful, who will not suffer you to be tempted above that ye are able; but will with the temptation also make a way to escape.\" The Greek for a way to escape is ekbasis, an exit, a way out of a narrow place, like a pass through mountains. Every temptation has a door. Your task is to walk through it, often by literally leaving the room, closing the screen or calling a friend.","Jesus' own method in the wilderness was the Word: \"It is written.\" Three times He answered the devil from Deuteronomy (Matthew 4). Psalm 119:11 says, \"Thy word have I hid in mine heart, that I might not sin against thee.\" Prepare before the battle: memorise Scripture, avoid places of weakness, keep accountable friends, and flee. The Bible does not tell us to stand and debate with some temptations. It tells us to flee (1 Corinthians 6:18; 2 Timothy 2:22). And if you fall, 1 John 1:9 stands open: confess, and He is faithful and just to forgive."],"christ":"Revealed in Christ: He was tempted in every way and did not sin, and because He did, He is able to succour those who are tempted (Hebrews 2:18). He has been where you are, and He has won where you lose. His victory is credited to you, and His Spirit is given to you for the fight.","word":"The word behind the word: \"succour\" in Hebrews 2:18 is the Greek boetheo, to run to a cry for help. When you cry, Jesus runs toward you. You do not need to be strong first. You need to call.","walk":"Walk it out: Name your weak spots: the time, the place, the device, the company. Remove one trigger today. Memorise one verse for the moment of temptation. Tell one trusted person where you struggle, and ask them to check on you.","scriptures":[{"ref":"1 Corinthians 10:13","text":"There hath no temptation taken you but such as is common to man: but God is faithful, who will not suffer you to be tempted above that ye are able; but will with the temptation also make a way to escape, that ye may be able to bear it."},{"ref":"James 1:12-15","text":"Blessed is the man that endureth temptation: for when he is tried, he shall receive the crown of life, which the Lord hath promised to them that love him. Let no man say when he is tempted, I am tempted of God: for God cannot be tempted with evil, neither tempteth he any man: But every man is tempted, when he is drawn away of his own lust, and enticed. Then when lust hath conceived, it bringeth forth sin: and sin, when it is finished, bringeth forth death."},{"ref":"Hebrews 4:15-16","text":"For we have not an high priest which cannot be touched with the feeling of our infirmities; but was in all points tempted like as we are, yet without sin. Let us therefore come boldly unto the throne of grace, that we may obtain mercy, and find grace to help in time of need."},{"ref":"Matthew 26:41","text":"Watch and pray, that ye enter not into temptation: the spirit indeed is willing, but the flesh is weak."},{"ref":"Psalm 119:9-11","text":"BETH. Wherewithal shall a young man cleanse his way? by taking heed thereto according to thy word. With my whole heart have I sought thee: O let me not wander from thy commandments. Thy word have I hid in mine heart, that I might not sin against thee."},{"ref":"Ephesians 6:11","text":"Put on the whole armour of God, that ye may be able to stand against the wiles of the devil."}],"prayers":[{"title":"Lord, lead me away from temptation","text":"Father, You taught us to pray, \"lead us not into temptation, but deliver us from evil.\" I pray it now. You know where I am weak. You know the thoughts that keep returning. I bring them into the light, because darkness is where they grow.\n\nGive me the grace to flee. Open the way of escape that You promised, and give me the courage to take it. Put Your Word into my mouth when I am pressed. Surround me with believers who will ask me hard questions. Strengthen my will, and renew my desires so that I long for what is holy more than what is harmful.\n\nI put on the armour of God: truth, righteousness, peace, faith, salvation and the Word. Jesus, You overcame, and I stand in Your victory. Where I have fallen, forgive me and wash me clean. Let my failures drive me to You and not away from You. In Jesus' name, Amen."},{"title":"Father, in the moment of weakness","text":"Lord, I am close to a line tonight. I can feel the pull. I call on You right now, as the one who runs to a cry for help. Come quickly. Make me strong where I am weak.\n\nI renounce this temptation in the name of Jesus. I will not give it a place. I choose to leave, to look away, to call a friend, to open Your Word. Fill my mind with what is pure, lovely and of good report. Give me the joy of obedience that is greater than the pleasure of sin.\n\nThank You that Your mercy is new every morning, and that a fall does not end the story. Make me faithful. Keep me watching and praying. In Jesus' name, Amen."}],"declare":["God is faithful and provides a way out.","Greater is He that is in me than he that is in the world.","I resist the devil, and he flees from me.","I am more than a conqueror through Christ."]},"peace":{"doc":["Peace in the Bible is much more than quiet. The Hebrew shalom means wholeness, completeness, welfare: everything being as God intends. It is used for greeting and blessing because it wishes a person the fullness of well-being. When Jesus says, \"Peace I leave with you, my peace I give unto you: not as the world giveth, give I unto you\" (John 14:27), He gives His own settled wholeness, not an absence of problems.","Isaiah 26:3 says, \"Thou wilt keep him in perfect peace, whose mind is stayed on thee: because he trusteth in thee.\" In Hebrew the phrase is shalom shalom, peace, peace, a doubling that means peace upon peace. The word for stayed, samakh, means to lean, to rest your full weight on something. Peace comes when your mind leans its whole weight on God, rather than on circumstances.","Romans 5:1 gives the foundation: \"Therefore being justified by faith, we have peace with God through our Lord Jesus Christ.\" Before there can be the peace of God, there must be peace with God. The enmity was ended at the cross. Colossians 1:20 says He \"made peace through the blood of his cross.\" Everything else flows from this. A person who has been reconciled to God has a ground of peace that no shifting circumstance can remove.","Philippians 4:7 says that \"the peace of God, which passeth all understanding, shall keep your hearts and minds through Christ Jesus.\" It passes understanding: it does not make sense by the world's logic. Peace in a hospital room, peace in loss, peace in uncertainty. It is a fruit of the Spirit (Galatians 5:22), grown, not forced. And Colossians 3:15 says to let it \"rule\" in your hearts. The word rule is brabeuo, to act as umpire. When voices clash inside you, let peace make the call."],"christ":"Revealed in Christ: He is called the Prince of Peace (Isaiah 9:6). He slept in the storm, and He stilled it with three words: \"Peace, be still\" (Mark 4:39). The same voice speaks to your inner storm. After the resurrection, His first word to the frightened disciples was, \"Peace be unto you\" (John 20:19). Peace is a Person who walks into the room.","word":"The word behind the word: \"shalom\" comes from a root meaning to be whole or to pay in full. In Christ the debt is paid in full, and so your peace is the peace of a settled account.","walk":"Walk it out: Sit still for two minutes. Breathe slowly. Pray Isaiah 26:3 back to God, replacing \"him\" with \"me.\" Hand over the one thing that is stealing your peace. Then do one small, kind thing for someone else.","scriptures":[{"ref":"John 14:27","text":"Peace I leave with you, my peace I give unto you: not as the world giveth, give I unto you. Let not your heart be troubled, neither let it be afraid."},{"ref":"Isaiah 26:3","text":"Thou wilt keep him in perfect peace, whose mind is stayed on thee: because he trusteth in thee."},{"ref":"Philippians 4:6-7","text":"Be careful for nothing; but in every thing by prayer and supplication with thanksgiving let your requests be made known unto God. And the peace of God, which passeth all understanding, shall keep your hearts and minds through Christ Jesus."},{"ref":"Colossians 3:15","text":"And let the peace of God rule in your hearts, to the which also ye are called in one body; and be ye thankful."},{"ref":"Romans 5:1","text":"Therefore being justified by faith, we have peace with God through our Lord Jesus Christ:"},{"ref":"Numbers 6:24-26","text":"The LORD bless thee, and keep thee: The LORD make his face shine upon thee, and be gracious unto thee: The LORD lift up his countenance upon thee, and give thee peace."},{"ref":"Psalm 4:8","text":"I will both lay me down in peace, and sleep: for thou, LORD, only makest me dwell in safety."}],"prayers":[{"title":"Prince of Peace, rule my heart","text":"Lord Jesus, Prince of Peace, I come to You tired of the noise inside me. You said that Your peace is not like the world's peace. I ask for it now. Let it settle on my thoughts, my body, my home.\n\nI confess that I have leaned my weight on circumstances, on people and on my own plans, and they have shaken. Today I lean my whole weight on You. Keep my mind stayed on You, and keep me in perfect peace.\n\nWhere there is conflict, make me a peacemaker. Where there is fear, speak, \"Peace, be still.\" Let Your peace guard my heart and mind, and let it overflow to those around me. Thank You that I am at peace with God through You. In Jesus' name, Amen."},{"title":"Father, give me rest tonight","text":"Father, the day is ending and my mind is still running. I place the day in Your hands: what I did, what I did not do, the words I said, the worries I carry. I will not sleep on them. I give them to You.\n\nThank You for being awake when I am asleep. Thank You for being in control when I am not. Let Your peace wrap around me like a blanket. Quiet the voices of worry and regret. Let me lie down in peace, for You alone make me dwell in safety.\n\nWatch over my family, my home and the people I love. And let tomorrow find me rested and ready to walk with You. In Jesus' name, Amen."}],"declare":["The peace of God guards my heart and mind.","I am at peace with God through Jesus Christ.","My mind is stayed on Him.","I lie down and sleep in peace."]},"hope":{"doc":["Biblical hope is not wishful thinking. The Hebrew word tikvah comes from a root meaning a cord or line. In Joshua 2, Rahab tied a scarlet cord (tikvah) in her window, and that cord was her rescue. Hope is a cord that is tied to something real. Hebrews 6:19 calls it \"an anchor of the soul, both sure and stedfast, and which entereth into that within the veil.\" Your anchor is fixed not in the shifting sea but in the presence of God.","A second Hebrew word, qavah, means to wait, to twist together like strands of a rope. Isaiah 40:31 says, \"they that wait upon the Lord shall renew their strength.\" To wait on the Lord is to be braided into His strength until two strands become one. Hope is active, not idle. It stretches toward a promise, and the stretching makes the rope strong.","Romans 5:3-5 gives the chain: tribulation works patience, patience works experience, experience works hope, \"and hope maketh not ashamed; because the love of God is shed abroad in our hearts by the Holy Ghost.\" Suffering does not destroy hope when God's love is already poured into us. The proof is the cross: \"He that spared not his own Son... how shall he not with him also freely give us all things?\" (Romans 8:32).","Christian hope looks forward to a defined end: the return of Christ, the resurrection of the body, and a new heaven and new earth where God dwells with His people (1 Thessalonians 4:13-18; Revelation 21:1-5). This means no grave is the last word, no diagnosis, no failure, no loss. Paul says we sorrow, but not as others who have no hope. Hope does not remove tears; it surrounds them with a promise."],"christ":"Revealed in Christ: He is called \"Christ in you, the hope of glory\" (Colossians 1:27). Hope is a Person. The empty tomb is the guarantee that every promise will be kept, because the One who made them rose from the dead and is alive (1 Peter 1:3).","word":"The word behind the word: tikvah is the cord in Rahab's window. Look for the scarlet thread in your own story, the line of God's faithfulness that runs through the hardest times. Tie your hope to it.","walk":"Walk it out: Write down three times God has come through for you. Underline one promise from Scripture that fits your situation today. Say it aloud three times. Then tell someone who is discouraged about it.","scriptures":[{"ref":"Romans 15:13","text":"Now the God of hope fill you with all joy and peace in believing, that ye may abound in hope, through the power of the Holy Ghost."},{"ref":"Hebrews 6:18-19","text":"That by two immutable things, in which it was impossible for God to lie, we might have a strong consolation, who have fled for refuge to lay hold upon the hope set before us: Which hope we have as an anchor of the soul, both sure and stedfast, and which entereth into that within the veil;"},{"ref":"Lamentations 3:21-23","text":"This I recall to my mind, therefore have I hope. It is of the LORD'S mercies that we are not consumed, because his compassions fail not. They are new every morning: great is thy faithfulness."},{"ref":"Isaiah 40:31","text":"But they that wait upon the LORD shall renew their strength; they shall mount up with wings as eagles; they shall run, and not be weary; and they shall walk, and not faint."},{"ref":"Jeremiah 29:11","text":"For I know the thoughts that I think toward you, saith the LORD, thoughts of peace, and not of evil, to give you an expected end."},{"ref":"Romans 5:3-5","text":"And not only so, but we glory in tribulations also: knowing that tribulation worketh patience; And patience, experience; and experience, hope: And hope maketh not ashamed; because the love of God is shed abroad in our hearts by the Holy Ghost which is given unto us."},{"ref":"1 Peter 1:3","text":"Blessed be the God and Father of our Lord Jesus Christ, which according to his abundant mercy hath begotten us again unto a lively hope by the resurrection of Jesus Christ from the dead,"}],"prayers":[{"title":"God of hope, fill me","text":"Father, You are the God of hope. I confess that I have let circumstances tell me that hope is gone. I bring You my disappointment, my waiting and my unanswered prayers. Fill me with all joy and peace in believing, that I may abound in hope by the power of the Holy Spirit.\n\nTie my heart to Your promises like a cord to an anchor. Let me not be driven by every wave. Remind me that Jesus is alive, that the grave has lost, and that You are working even where I cannot see.\n\nRenew my strength as I wait on You. Give me eyes for the new things You are springing up. Let me be a person who carries hope to others, speaking life in dark places. Thank You that Your mercies are new every morning and great is Your faithfulness. In Jesus' name, Amen."},{"title":"Lord, when hope is thin","text":"Lord, tonight hope is thin. I do not have the words, but You know my heart. I ask You to be the strength of my heart and my portion forever.\n\nPlease send someone to encourage me, or let a verse find me. Let me remember something good You have done. Lift my head. Remind me that weeping may endure for a night, but joy comes in the morning.\n\nI choose to wait on You. I choose to believe that You are good, that You are near, and that You are at work. Do not let me be put to shame. Let my hope rest on You alone. In Jesus' name, Amen."}],"declare":["Christ in me is my hope of glory.","My hope is an anchor for my soul.","I wait on the Lord and my strength is renewed.","God's mercies are new every morning."]},"strength":{"doc":["The Bible's strength is not self-made. Psalm 46:1 says, \"God is our refuge and strength, a very present help in trouble.\" Strength here is a gift, found in a Person. Paul learned the lesson in a hard way when he asked three times to be rid of his thorn and heard, \"My strength is made perfect in weakness\" (2 Corinthians 12:9). His conclusion is startling: \"when I am weak, then am I strong.\" It is when we stop pretending to be strong that His strength becomes visible.","Isaiah 40:31 promises, \"they that wait upon the Lord shall renew their strength.\" The Hebrew verb for renew, chalaph, means to exchange, as one changes clothes. We swap our strength for His. You give Him tiredness, and He gives you stamina. The context is important: Isaiah says even the youths faint and fall (verse 30). No human strength is enough. Only those who wait on God mount up with wings.","Philippians 4:13 is often quoted: \"I can do all things through Christ which strengtheneth me.\" Its context is contentment in both plenty and hunger. The strength Paul speaks of is not the power to win every ambition but the power to endure and to be content in any condition. The Greek endunamoo means to pour power into someone. It is a continuous empowering, not a one-off.","Ephesians 6:10 commands, \"be strong in the Lord, and in the power of his might.\" The strength is located in Him. Then come the armour pieces: truth, righteousness, the gospel of peace, faith, salvation and the Word. Strength is also found in the community, for \"a threefold cord is not quickly broken\" (Ecclesiastes 4:12). Rest is part of strength: Jesus withdrew to quiet places, and even the strongest need Sabbath. Do not be ashamed to ask for help."],"christ":"Revealed in Christ: He is \"the power of God, and the wisdom of God\" (1 Corinthians 1:24). The strongest moment in history looks like weakness: a man on a cross. In that weakness He broke death. The power that raised Him from the dead lives in those who believe (Ephesians 1:19-20).","word":"The word behind the word: \"renew\" in Isaiah 40:31 is chalaph, to exchange or to pass on through. It is the word for sprouting a fresh shoot from a stump, and for putting on new clothes. Your strength is not repaired; it is replaced.","walk":"Walk it out: Tell the Lord the area where you feel weakest. Say aloud, \"I give You my weakness, give me Your strength.\" Then do the next small task. Get rest and a proper meal, and ask a friend for help with something heavy.","scriptures":[{"ref":"Isaiah 40:29-31","text":"He giveth power to the faint; and to them that have no might he increaseth strength. Even the youths shall faint and be weary, and the young men shall utterly fall: But they that wait upon the LORD shall renew their strength; they shall mount up with wings as eagles; they shall run, and not be weary; and they shall walk, and not faint."},{"ref":"Psalm 46:1","text":"To the chief Musician for the sons of Korah, A Song upon Alamoth. God is our refuge and strength, a very present help in trouble."},{"ref":"Philippians 4:13","text":"I can do all things through Christ which strengtheneth me."},{"ref":"2 Corinthians 12:9-10","text":"And he said unto me, My grace is sufficient for thee: for my strength is made perfect in weakness. Most gladly therefore will I rather glory in my infirmities, that the power of Christ may rest upon me. Therefore I take pleasure in infirmities, in reproaches, in necessities, in persecutions, in distresses for Christ's sake: for when I am weak, then am I strong."},{"ref":"Ephesians 6:10","text":"Finally, my brethren, be strong in the Lord, and in the power of his might."},{"ref":"Nehemiah 8:10","text":"Then he said unto them, Go your way, eat the fat, and drink the sweet, and send portions unto them for whom nothing is prepared: for this day is holy unto our Lord: neither be ye sorry; for the joy of the LORD is your strength."},{"ref":"Psalm 28:7","text":"The LORD is my strength and my shield; my heart trusted in him, and I am helped: therefore my heart greatly rejoiceth; and with my song will I praise him."}],"prayers":[{"title":"Lord, be my strength","text":"Father, I am tired, and I do not have enough in myself. I give You my weakness. Exchange my strength for Yours, as You promised. Let me mount up with wings as eagles, run and not be weary, walk and not faint.\n\nI thank You that Your strength is made perfect in weakness. Teach me to boast not in what I can do but in what You do through me. Where I am carrying more than I should, show me what to put down. Where I need help, give me the humility to ask.\n\nFill me with the joy of the Lord, which is my strength. Pour power into me as You did into Paul. Help me to stand in the evil day, clothed in Your armour. Be my rock, my fortress and my high tower. In Jesus' name, Amen."},{"title":"Father, I am worn out","text":"Lord, I am worn thin. Some days I do not know how to keep going. I bring You my exhaustion, my responsibilities and the people who lean on me.\n\nGive me rest that goes deeper than sleep. Give me the strength for today only, since You promised strength equal to my days. Show me what is mine to do and what is Yours. Send me help in the form of kindness, wisdom and community.\n\nI wait on You. I lift my eyes to the hills, knowing that my help comes from You. Hold me up with Your righteous right hand. Let me rise again, not in my own power, but in Yours. In Jesus' name, Amen."}],"declare":["The Lord is my strength and my shield.","I can do all things through Christ who strengthens me.","When I am weak, He is strong.","I wait on the Lord and I do not faint."]},"identity":{"doc":["Who you are is settled by what God says, not by what others have said or by what you have done. Genesis 1:27 says we are made \"in the image of God.\" Every person bears His likeness, which gives worth that no failure can erase. Psalm 139:14 adds, \"I am fearfully and wonderfully made.\" You were not a mistake. Your life was intended.","In Christ the identity is made new. 2 Corinthians 5:17 says, \"if any man be in Christ, he is a new creature: old things are passed away; behold, all things are become new.\" The Greek is kaine ktisis, a new creation, new in kind and not merely renovated. John 1:12 says, \"as many as received him, to them gave he power to become the sons of God.\" You are not a servant on probation; you are a child.","Paul uses the word huiothesia, adoption as sons, in Romans 8:15 and Galatians 4:5-7. In Roman law an adopted son was fully an heir, with all debts cleared and a new name. It was a legal act with lasting effect. Because of it, \"ye have received the Spirit of adoption, whereby we cry, Abba, Father.\" Abba is the intimate address of a child to a father. Orphans of this world become sons and daughters of the King.","Ephesians 1 and 2 list what is true of you: chosen, adopted, accepted, redeemed, forgiven, sealed, seated with Christ, and made alive. 1 Peter 2:9 calls believers \"a chosen generation, a royal priesthood, an holy nation, a peculiar people.\" You can lay down labels: failure, rejected, too much, not enough. The enemy accuses; Christ declares. Let His voice be the loudest. Identity is received, not earned. It is rooted in His love, and it produces confidence, humility and service."],"christ":"Revealed in Christ: at His baptism, before He did a single miracle, the Father said, \"This is my beloved Son, in whom I am well pleased\" (Matthew 3:17). Sonship came before service. In Christ, the same love is spoken over you, before and apart from your performance.","word":"The word behind the word: \"workmanship\" in Ephesians 2:10 is poiema, a work of art, and \"new creature\" is kaine ktisis, a new creation. You are not patched; you are made new, and made on purpose.","walk":"Walk it out: Write down one label you carry that God did not give you. Next to it, write a verse that tells the truth. Read the verse aloud each morning this week. When the old label returns, answer it with the verse.","scriptures":[{"ref":"Genesis 1:27","text":"So God created man in his own image, in the image of God created he him; male and female created he them."},{"ref":"Psalm 139:13-16","text":"For thou hast possessed my reins: thou hast covered me in my mother's womb. I will praise thee; for I am fearfully and wonderfully made: marvellous are thy works; and that my soul knoweth right well. My substance was not hid from thee, when I was made in secret, and curiously wrought in the lowest parts of the earth. Thine eyes did see my substance, yet being unperfect; and in thy book all my members were written, which in continuance were fashioned, when as yet there was none of them."},{"ref":"2 Corinthians 5:17","text":"Therefore if any man be in Christ, he is a new creature: old things are passed away; behold, all things are become new."},{"ref":"John 1:12","text":"But as many as received him, to them gave he power to become the sons of God, even to them that believe on his name:"},{"ref":"Romans 8:15-17","text":"For ye have not received the spirit of bondage again to fear; but ye have received the Spirit of adoption, whereby we cry, Abba, Father. The Spirit itself beareth witness with our spirit, that we are the children of God: And if children, then heirs; heirs of God, and joint-heirs with Christ; if so be that we suffer with him, that we may be also glorified together."},{"ref":"1 Peter 2:9","text":"But ye are a chosen generation, a royal priesthood, an holy nation, a peculiar people; that ye should shew forth the praises of him who hath called you out of darkness into his marvellous light:"},{"ref":"Ephesians 1:4-6","text":"According as he hath chosen us in him before the foundation of the world, that we should be holy and without blame before him in love: Having predestinated us unto the adoption of children by Jesus Christ to himself, according to the good pleasure of his will, To the praise of the glory of his grace, wherein he hath made us accepted in the beloved."}],"prayers":[{"title":"Father, tell me who I am","text":"Father, I thank You that You made me in Your image and that You know me by name. I come to You tired of the voices that tell me who I am: the voice of the past, the voice of other people, the voice of my own failures. I lay them down.\n\nSpeak over me what You say. Let me hear that I am Your child, chosen, loved and accepted in Christ. Help me to believe it, not only in my head but in my bones. Let the Spirit of adoption cry \"Abba, Father\" in me.\n\nHeal the places in me that were told I was not enough. Free me from the need to perform for love. Let me walk with a quiet confidence that does not need to prove itself or put others down. Make me a new creation, living from Your love. In Jesus' name, Amen."},{"title":"Lord, deliver me from comparison","text":"Lord, I compare myself to others, and I always lose. Forgive me for envy and for despising what You made in me. You made me on purpose, and You gave me gifts and a story that are mine.\n\nTeach me to celebrate others without shrinking myself. Silence the accuser, who reminds me of what I have done. Speak the finished work of Jesus over me, that there is no condemnation for those who are in Christ.\n\nSettle me in my place in Your family. Let me live from acceptance, not for acceptance. And let others who look at my life see how good it is to be Your child. In Jesus' name, Amen."}],"declare":["I am a child of God.","I am a new creation in Christ.","I am fearfully and wonderfully made.","I am chosen, loved and accepted."]},"gratitude":{"doc":["Gratitude is commanded in Scripture because it is good for us: \"In every thing give thanks: for this is the will of God in Christ Jesus concerning you\" (1 Thessalonians 5:18). It says in everything, not for everything. We do not thank God for evil, but we thank Him in it, because He is present in it and working in it. Thanks is an act of faith that declares that God is still good.","The Greek word for giving thanks is eucharisteo, from eu (good) and charis (grace). Thanks is a response to grace. It is also the root of our word Eucharist, because Jesus \"gave thanks\" before breaking the bread (Luke 22:19), and He did so on the night before His death. If He could give thanks on that night, we can learn to give thanks in ours.","The Hebrew word todah, thanksgiving, comes from a root meaning to extend the hand. It is the open hand of thanks. It was the name of a sacrifice (Psalm 50:14, 23; Psalm 107:22). Thanks is an offering. Psalm 100 gives the way in: \"Enter into his gates with thanksgiving, and into his courts with praise.\" Thanks opens the door to His presence.","Ingratitude, in contrast, begins the spiral in Romans 1:21: \"when they knew God, they glorified him not as God, neither were thankful.\" Grumbling shrinks the heart. The Israelites in the wilderness complained about the very mercies that rescued them. Gratitude trains the eyes to see the gift. It also protects against comparison, entitlement and anxiety, because a thankful heart is a full one. Count what you have, and remember what He has done."],"christ":"Revealed in Christ: He thanked the Father before the miracle (John 11:41), before the meal (Matthew 15:36) and before the cross (Luke 22:19). Thanks was His posture of trust. He is also the greatest gift, \"Thanks be unto God for his unspeakable gift\" (2 Corinthians 9:15). All other gratitude flows from this.","word":"The word behind the word: eucharisteo holds charis, grace, inside it. Thanks is grace recognised. The more you notice grace, the more you thank, and the more you thank, the more you notice.","walk":"Walk it out: Write three things you are thankful for each day this week, and make each different. Thank one person by name, in writing or in person. Thank God for something that you once prayed for and now have.","scriptures":[{"ref":"1 Thessalonians 5:16-18","text":"Rejoice evermore. Pray without ceasing. In every thing give thanks: for this is the will of God in Christ Jesus concerning you."},{"ref":"Psalm 100:1-5","text":"A Psalm of praise. Make a joyful noise unto the LORD, all ye lands. Serve the LORD with gladness: come before his presence with singing. Know ye that the LORD he is God: it is he that hath made us, and not we ourselves; we are his people, and the sheep of his pasture. Enter into his gates with thanksgiving, and into his courts with praise: be thankful unto him, and bless his name. For the LORD is good; his mercy is everlasting; and his truth endureth to all generations."},{"ref":"Psalm 103:1-5","text":"A Psalm of David. Bless the LORD, O my soul: and all that is within me, bless his holy name. Bless the LORD, O my soul, and forget not all his benefits: Who forgiveth all thine iniquities; who healeth all thy diseases; Who redeemeth thy life from destruction; who crowneth thee with lovingkindness and tender mercies; Who satisfieth thy mouth with good things; so that thy youth is renewed like the eagle's."},{"ref":"Colossians 3:15-17","text":"And let the peace of God rule in your hearts, to the which also ye are called in one body; and be ye thankful. Let the word of Christ dwell in you richly in all wisdom; teaching and admonishing one another in psalms and hymns and spiritual songs, singing with grace in your hearts to the Lord. And whatsoever ye do in word or deed, do all in the name of the Lord Jesus, giving thanks to God and the Father by him."},{"ref":"Philippians 4:6","text":"Be careful for nothing; but in every thing by prayer and supplication with thanksgiving let your requests be made known unto God."},{"ref":"Psalm 107:1","text":"O give thanks unto the LORD, for he is good: for his mercy endureth for ever."},{"ref":"Lamentations 3:22-23","text":"It is of the LORD'S mercies that we are not consumed, because his compassions fail not. They are new every morning: great is thy faithfulness."}],"prayers":[{"title":"Father, I give You thanks","text":"Father, I thank You. Before I ask for anything, I thank You for life, for breath, for this day. Thank You for the roof over me, the food on my table, the people who love me, the Bible in my hand. Thank You for the things I take for granted.\n\nForgive me for the times I have complained about the very mercies that You gave. Open my eyes to see small gifts: a kind word, a clear morning, a safe journey, a healed relationship. Thank You most of all for Jesus, Your unspeakable gift, who bore my sin and gave me life.\n\nLet my life be a thank offering. Let my mouth speak praise and my hands give freely. Teach me to give thanks in everything, knowing that You are good and Your mercy endures forever. In Jesus' name, Amen."},{"title":"Lord, teach me to thank You in the hard times","text":"Lord, it is hard to be thankful tonight. Some things in my life are not as I wanted. I will not pretend. But I choose to bring You a sacrifice of thanksgiving, even if it costs me.\n\nI thank You that You are with me. I thank You that this will not last forever. I thank You that You have carried me before, and that Your mercies are new each morning. I thank You for the people who stood by me, and for the lessons that came only through pain.\n\nLet thankfulness lift my eyes from what is missing to what You have given. Turn my complaining into praise. And let gratitude grow into joy that no circumstance can take away. In Jesus' name, Amen."}],"declare":["I give thanks in every circumstance.","The Lord's mercies are new every morning.","I enter His gates with thanksgiving.","My heart is full of gratitude to God."]},"prayer":{"doc":["Prayer is conversation with the God who hears. It is not a technique to manipulate Him. It is a relationship with a Father. Jesus' first instruction in Matthew 6:6 is to go into a private place, shut the door, and pray \"to thy Father which is in secret.\" The word is Father. Prayer starts with the fact that He is near and that He wants to hear from you.","The Hebrew verb palal, to pray, is reflexive. It means to judge oneself, or to intercede. In prayer we are examined and aligned as well as heard. The Greek proseuchomai joins pros (toward) with euchomai (to wish or vow). Prayer is a turning toward God. And Jesus taught that persistence matters. In Luke 11:9 the verbs are in the present tense: \"Ask, and it shall be given you; seek, and ye shall find; knock, and it shall be opened.\" Keep asking, keep seeking, keep knocking.","The model prayer in Matthew 6:9-13 gives a shape: honour the Father's name, pray for His kingdom and will, ask for daily bread, ask for forgiveness and give it, ask for protection. Notice that it begins with God, and moves to our needs. The Spirit helps us when we do not know what to pray: \"the Spirit itself maketh intercession for us with groanings which cannot be uttered\" (Romans 8:26). Even your sighs count.","Prayer is not always answered the way we ask. Answers can be yes, no, wait or something better. Jesus Himself prayed, \"if it be possible, let this cup pass from me: nevertheless not as I will, but as thou wilt\" (Matthew 26:39). Sin can hinder prayer (Psalm 66:18), as can unforgiveness (Mark 11:25) and wrong motives (James 4:3). But the open door remains: \"Let us therefore come boldly unto the throne of grace\" (Hebrews 4:16). Pray in faith, in Jesus' name, in the Spirit, and in line with His Word."],"christ":"Revealed in Christ: \"he ever liveth to make intercession for them\" (Hebrews 7:25). While you pray, Jesus prays for you. Your prayers rise through the High Priest. He also prayed early in the morning (Mark 1:35) and all night (Luke 6:12), teaching us to prize time with the Father.","word":"The word behind the word: In Luke 11:9 \"ask, seek, knock\" are present imperatives, which means keep on asking, keep on seeking, keep on knocking. Prayer is persistence in relationship, not a single request.","walk":"Walk it out: Choose a regular time and place. Use the Lord's Prayer as a pattern, one line at a time. Pray for three people by name. Keep a short prayer list, and write down answers when they come.","scriptures":[{"ref":"Matthew 6:6-13","text":"But thou, when thou prayest, enter into thy closet, and when thou hast shut thy door, pray to thy Father which is in secret; and thy Father which seeth in secret shall reward thee openly. But when ye pray, use not vain repetitions, as the heathen do: for they think that they shall be heard for their much speaking. Be not ye therefore like unto them: for your Father knoweth what things ye have need of, before ye ask him. After this manner therefore pray ye: Our Father which art in heaven, Hallowed be thy name. Thy kingdom come. Thy will be done in earth, as it is in heaven. Give us this day our daily bread. And forgive us our debts, as we forgive our debtors. And lead us not into temptation, but deliver us from evil: For thine is the kingdom, and the power, and the glory, for ever. Amen."},{"ref":"Philippians 4:6-7","text":"Be careful for nothing; but in every thing by prayer and supplication with thanksgiving let your requests be made known unto God. And the peace of God, which passeth all understanding, shall keep your hearts and minds through Christ Jesus."},{"ref":"Luke 11:9-10","text":"And I say unto you, Ask, and it shall be given you; seek, and ye shall find; knock, and it shall be opened unto you. For every one that asketh receiveth; and he that seeketh findeth; and to him that knocketh it shall be opened."},{"ref":"Romans 8:26","text":"Likewise the Spirit also helpeth our infirmities: for we know not what we should pray for as we ought: but the Spirit itself maketh intercession for us with groanings which cannot be uttered."},{"ref":"James 5:16","text":"Confess your faults one to another, and pray one for another, that ye may be healed. The effectual fervent prayer of a righteous man availeth much."},{"ref":"Hebrews 4:16","text":"Let us therefore come boldly unto the throne of grace, that we may obtain mercy, and find grace to help in time of need."},{"ref":"Jeremiah 33:3","text":"Call unto me, and I will answer thee, and shew thee great and mighty things, which thou knowest not."}],"prayers":[{"title":"Lord, teach me to pray","text":"Lord Jesus, as Your disciples asked, I ask: teach me to pray. Draw me out of hurried words into real conversation. Make me hungry for time with You, more than for the things I ask.\n\nOur Father in heaven, hallowed be Your name. Your kingdom come, Your will be done in my life, my family and my nation, as it is in heaven. Give me today my daily bread. Forgive me my sins as I forgive those who sinned against me. Lead me not into temptation, but deliver me from evil.\n\nSend Your Spirit to pray through me when I have no words. Make me persistent and patient, trusting that You hear. Cleanse my heart from anything that blocks my prayers. Let my life become a prayer. In Jesus' name, Amen."},{"title":"Father, I bring others to You","text":"Father, I stand in the gap for others. I bring to You my family, my friends, my pastor, my leaders, my neighbours and those who do not know You. I name them before You, one by one.\n\nWhere they are sick, heal. Where they are lost, find. Where they are afraid, comfort. Where they are tempted, strengthen. Send workers into the harvest. Break the chains of those who are held in darkness, and bring them into the light of Christ.\n\nGive me love that cares enough to pray, and faith that waits for Your answer. Thank You that the prayer of a righteous person is powerful and effective. Use my prayers to move mountains, and use my life to answer them. In Jesus' name, Amen."}],"declare":["I come boldly to the throne of grace.","The Lord hears me when I call.","The Spirit helps me when I do not know what to pray.","I pray without ceasing."]},"love":{"doc":["The Bible does not merely say God loves; it says \"God is love\" (1 John 4:8). Love is not one of God's moods but His nature. The love He shows is agape, the Greek word for a self-giving love that chooses the other's good at its own cost. John 3:16 shows its shape: \"God so loved the world, that he gave.\" Love gives. Romans 5:8 adds, \"while we were yet sinners, Christ died for us.\" It does not wait for us to deserve it.","The Hebrew word chesed, often translated lovingkindness or steadfast love or mercy, is covenant loyalty. It is love that keeps its promises. Psalm 136 repeats 26 times, \"for his mercy endureth for ever.\" The word is chesed each time. His love is not a feeling that comes and goes. It is a commitment that holds when we fail.","1 Corinthians 13 is the portrait. Paul says that without love, prophecy, knowledge, faith and giving are nothing. Then he describes it with verbs: love is patient (makrothumei, long-tempered), kind, does not envy, does not boast, is not rude, does not insist on its own way, is not irritable, keeps no record of wrongs, rejoices in truth, bears, believes, hopes and endures. \"Charity never faileth.\" When you want to know whether you love, read this list and see where it presses.","Jesus summed up the law: love God with all your heart, soul, mind and strength, and love your neighbour as yourself (Mark 12:30-31). He gave a new commandment: \"love one another; as I have loved you\" (John 13:34). The measure is Him. 1 John 4:19 says, \"We love him, because he first loved us.\" We do not produce love from nothing; we receive it and pass it on. Those who know they are loved can love others, even enemies (Matthew 5:44)."],"christ":"Revealed in Christ: \"Greater love hath no man than this, that a man lay down his life for his friends\" (John 15:13). He did. In Him love is not an idea but a body on a cross and a risen Lord. He washed feet, touched lepers, ate with outcasts and forgave His killers. Whatever love is, He is.","word":"The word behind the word: in 1 Corinthians 13:4 \"suffereth long\" is makrothumei, literally long-tempered, a long fuse. Love has a long fuse. It also uses the Greek verb form for ongoing action: love keeps on being patient.","walk":"Walk it out: Read 1 Corinthians 13:4-7 and put your own name in place of the word love. Where does it hurt? Choose one person to love in a practical way today, with a kind word, a call, or a meal. Do it without expecting anything.","scriptures":[{"ref":"1 John 4:7-12","text":"Beloved, let us love one another: for love is of God; and every one that loveth is born of God, and knoweth God. He that loveth not knoweth not God; for God is love. In this was manifested the love of God toward us, because that God sent his only begotten Son into the world, that we might live through him. Herein is love, not that we loved God, but that he loved us, and sent his Son to be the propitiation for our sins. Beloved, if God so loved us, we ought also to love one another. No man hath seen God at any time. If we love one another, God dwelleth in us, and his love is perfected in us."},{"ref":"John 3:16","text":"For God so loved the world, that he gave his only begotten Son, that whosoever believeth in him should not perish, but have everlasting life."},{"ref":"Romans 5:8","text":"But God commendeth his love toward us, in that, while we were yet sinners, Christ died for us."},{"ref":"1 Corinthians 13:4-8","text":"Charity suffereth long, and is kind; charity envieth not; charity vaunteth not itself, is not puffed up, Doth not behave itself unseemly, seeketh not her own, is not easily provoked, thinketh no evil; Rejoiceth not in iniquity, but rejoiceth in the truth; Beareth all things, believeth all things, hopeth all things, endureth all things. Charity never faileth: but whether there be prophecies, they shall fail; whether there be tongues, they shall cease; whether there be knowledge, it shall vanish away."},{"ref":"John 13:34-35","text":"A new commandment I give unto you, That ye love one another; as I have loved you, that ye also love one another. By this shall all men know that ye are my disciples, if ye have love one to another."},{"ref":"Romans 8:38-39","text":"For I am persuaded, that neither death, nor life, nor angels, nor principalities, nor powers, nor things present, nor things to come, Nor height, nor depth, nor any other creature, shall be able to separate us from the love of God, which is in Christ Jesus our Lord."},{"ref":"Matthew 22:37-39","text":"Jesus said unto him, Thou shalt love the Lord thy God with all thy heart, and with all thy soul, and with all thy mind. This is the first and great commandment. And the second is like unto it, Thou shalt love thy neighbour as thyself."}],"prayers":[{"title":"God of love, fill me","text":"Father, You are love, and You loved me before I knew You. I thank You for Jesus, who laid down His life for me. Pour Your love into my heart by the Holy Spirit until it overflows.\n\nForgive me where I have been selfish, cold or unkind. Soften what has hardened in me. Teach me the love that is patient, the love that keeps no record of wrongs, the love that bears all things. Show me who needs a kind word, a visit, a meal or my forgiveness today.\n\nHelp me to love the difficult people, and even those who have hurt me. Let me love not only with words but in deed and in truth. And let the world know that we are Your disciples by the love we have for one another. In Jesus' name, Amen."},{"title":"Lord, help me receive Your love","text":"Lord, I find it hard to believe that I am loved. I know the verses, but my heart does not always trust them. Make Your love real to me tonight.\n\nWhere I have been wounded by people who should have loved me, heal me. Where I have earned love by performing, free me. Let me know that nothing can separate me from Your love, not death, not life, not my failures, not my fears.\n\nRoot and ground me in love. Let me know its width, length, height and depth. Then let me share it freely, for what I have received without cost I can give without cost. In Jesus' name, Amen."}],"declare":["God is love, and I am loved by Him.","Nothing can separate me from the love of God.","Love is patient and kind in me.","I love because He first loved me."]},"patience":{"doc":["Patience is one of the hardest fruits of the Spirit (Galatians 5:22), and Scripture says it is grown under pressure. The Greek makrothumia, long-temperedness, describes patience with people. The Greek hupomone, from hupo (under) and meno (remain), describes patience with circumstances: the ability to remain under a load without running away. James 1:3-4 says, \"the trying of your faith worketh patience. But let patience have her perfect work, that ye may be perfect and entire, wanting nothing.\"","God is the model. Exodus 34:6 describes Him as \"slow to anger.\" The Hebrew is erek apayim, long of nostrils: a long breath before anger. 2 Peter 3:9 says He is longsuffering toward us, not willing that any should perish. The delay we sometimes resent in His promises is mercy giving time. So patience grows as we remember how patient He has been with us.","Waiting is often where God does His best work. Abraham waited twenty-five years for Isaac. Joseph waited thirteen years from the pit to the palace. Moses spent forty years in the desert. David waited years between the anointing and the throne. Habakkuk 2:3 says that the vision \"will surely come, it will not tarry\" and in the meantime \"wait for it.\" Waiting is not wasted; it is a workshop. Psalm 37:7 says, \"Rest in the Lord, and wait patiently for him.\"","Impatience reaches for shortcuts, and shortcuts cost: Abraham took Hagar, Saul offered the sacrifice himself, Moses struck the rock. Patience trusts God's timing and God's way. It also shows up in daily life: in traffic, in queues, in children, in marriage, in slow growth. Patience is love that refuses to hurry another person's change. Ask for it, practice it, and thank God for the situations that train it."],"christ":"Revealed in Christ: \"Consider him that endured such contradiction of sinners against himself, lest ye be wearied and faint in your minds\" (Hebrews 12:3). He endured the cross, despising the shame. He waited thirty years before He began His ministry. He is patient with your slow progress, and He teaches you to be patient with others.","word":"The word behind the word: hupomone is \"remaining under.\" It is the patience of a person who carries a load without throwing it down. It is the opposite of escaping, and it is used of the endurance of the saints (Revelation 14:12).","walk":"Walk it out: Name the situation in which you are most impatient. Ask God what He is doing in you while you wait. Do not take a shortcut today. Practice slowing down: breathe before you answer, and let the other person finish.","scriptures":[{"ref":"James 1:2-4","text":"My brethren, count it all joy when ye fall into divers temptations; Knowing this, that the trying of your faith worketh patience. But let patience have her perfect work, that ye may be perfect and entire, wanting nothing."},{"ref":"Psalm 37:7-9","text":"Rest in the LORD, and wait patiently for him: fret not thyself because of him who prospereth in his way, because of the man who bringeth wicked devices to pass. Cease from anger, and forsake wrath: fret not thyself in any wise to do evil. For evildoers shall be cut off: but those that wait upon the LORD, they shall inherit the earth."},{"ref":"Galatians 5:22-23","text":"But the fruit of the Spirit is love, joy, peace, longsuffering, gentleness, goodness, faith, Meekness, temperance: against such there is no law."},{"ref":"Romans 8:25","text":"But if we hope for that we see not, then do we with patience wait for it."},{"ref":"Isaiah 40:31","text":"But they that wait upon the LORD shall renew their strength; they shall mount up with wings as eagles; they shall run, and not be weary; and they shall walk, and not faint."},{"ref":"Habakkuk 2:3","text":"For the vision is yet for an appointed time, but at the end it shall speak, and not lie: though it tarry, wait for it; because it will surely come, it will not tarry."},{"ref":"Hebrews 12:1-2","text":"Wherefore seeing we also are compassed about with so great a cloud of witnesses, let us lay aside every weight, and the sin which doth so easily beset us, and let us run with patience the race that is set before us, Looking unto Jesus the author and finisher of our faith; who for the joy that was set before him endured the cross, despising the shame, and is set down at the right hand of the throne of God."}],"prayers":[{"title":"Lord, grow patience in me","text":"Father, I confess that I am impatient. I want things now, and I want people to change now. Forgive me for the times I have snapped, rushed or taken shortcuts. Thank You for being slow to anger and rich in mercy toward me.\n\nPlant in me the fruit of patience. Teach me to remain under the pressure without running. Let me trust Your timing, which is never late. Where I am waiting for an answer, a door, a healing or a change, hold me steady.\n\nGive me the long fuse of love for the people around me. Let me listen before I speak and bless before I react. Make me like Jesus, who endured and did not give up. In Jesus' name, Amen."},{"title":"Father, while I wait","text":"Father, I have been waiting a long time, and I am tired. I believe You are working, even when I cannot see it. Strengthen my heart. Let me not grow weary in doing good, for in due season I will reap if I do not faint.\n\nProtect me from bitterness and from doing things in my own way. Remind me of those who waited before me and were not disappointed. Use this season to deepen my roots.\n\nI rest in You and wait patiently. I trust that Your timing is perfect. Let me keep serving, praying and hoping until the answer comes. In Jesus' name, Amen."}],"declare":["The Lord is patient with me and I am patient with others.","I wait on the Lord and He renews my strength.","In due season I will reap if I do not faint.","God's timing is perfect."]},"conflict":{"doc":["Conflict is part of life among people, and the Bible does not hide it. The early church had disputes, Paul and Barnabas split over Mark, and Jesus gave a whole process for dealing with it (Matthew 18:15-17). The question is not whether conflict comes but how we handle it. Romans 12:18 says, \"If it be possible, as much as lieth in you, live peaceably with all men.\" Notice the two limits: if it is possible, and as much as depends on you. You are responsible for your part only.","Jesus gave a clear order. First, go to the person privately: \"tell him his fault between thee and him alone.\" Not to the group chat, not to the neighbours. If that fails, take one or two others. Only after that, the church. The aim, in the Lord's words, is \"thou hast gained thy brother.\" The goal is restoration, not victory. Before you go, Matthew 7:3-5 says to take the log out of your own eye first, so you can see clearly.","Proverbs gives wisdom for the tongue: \"A soft answer turneth away wrath: but grievous words stir up anger\" (15:1). \"He that answereth a matter before he heareth it, it is folly and shame unto him\" (18:13). James 1:19 says, \"swift to hear, slow to speak, slow to wrath.\" Ephesians 4:26 permits anger but limits it: \"Be ye angry, and sin not: let not the sun go down upon your wrath.\" Truth must be spoken, but in love (Ephesians 4:15).","Jesus said, \"Blessed are the peacemakers: for they shall be called the children of God\" (Matthew 5:9). The Greek eirenopoios means one who makes peace, an active role, not just one who avoids trouble. The peacemaker is not the peace-keeper who hides issues, but the one who brings reconciliation. And Matthew 5:23-24 says if you remember that someone has something against you, go first to be reconciled, before you bring your gift. Peace between people matters to God as much as worship."],"christ":"Revealed in Christ: \"He is our peace, who hath made both one, and hath broken down the middle wall of partition\" (Ephesians 2:14). The cross reconciled us to God and to each other. When He was reviled, He did not revile again (1 Peter 2:23). On the cross He prayed, \"Father, forgive them.\" His way of handling conflict is the way of the cross.","word":"The word behind the word: eirenopoios, peacemaker, is made of eirene (peace) and poieo (to make). The same poieo gives us poiema, workmanship. Peacemakers are craftsmen: they construct peace with patient hands.","walk":"Walk it out: Think of one unresolved conflict. Pray for the person by name for a week. Ask what your part is. Then take the first step privately: a message, a call or a visit. Begin with, \"I value you, and I want us to be well.\"","scriptures":[{"ref":"Matthew 18:15","text":"Moreover if thy brother shall trespass against thee, go and tell him his fault between thee and him alone: if he shall hear thee, thou hast gained thy brother."},{"ref":"Matthew 5:23-24","text":"Therefore if thou bring thy gift to the altar, and there rememberest that thy brother hath ought against thee; Leave there thy gift before the altar, and go thy way; first be reconciled to thy brother, and then come and offer thy gift."},{"ref":"Romans 12:17-21","text":"Recompense to no man evil for evil. Provide things honest in the sight of all men. If it be possible, as much as lieth in you, live peaceably with all men. Dearly beloved, avenge not yourselves, but rather give place unto wrath: for it is written, Vengeance is mine; I will repay, saith the Lord. Therefore if thine enemy hunger, feed him; if he thirst, give him drink: for in so doing thou shalt heap coals of fire on his head. Be not overcome of evil, but overcome evil with good."},{"ref":"Proverbs 15:1","text":"A soft answer turneth away wrath: but grievous words stir up anger."},{"ref":"James 1:19-20","text":"Wherefore, my beloved brethren, let every man be swift to hear, slow to speak, slow to wrath: For the wrath of man worketh not the righteousness of God."},{"ref":"Ephesians 4:26-27","text":"Be ye angry, and sin not: let not the sun go down upon your wrath: Neither give place to the devil."},{"ref":"Ephesians 4:31-32","text":"Let all bitterness, and wrath, and anger, and clamour, and evil speaking, be put away from you, with all malice: And be ye kind one to another, tenderhearted, forgiving one another, even as God for Christ's sake hath forgiven you."}],"prayers":[{"title":"Lord, make me a peacemaker","text":"Father, You sent Your Son to reconcile us to You, and You gave us the ministry of reconciliation. I bring to You a broken or strained relationship. You know the details, and You know my part.\n\nShow me where I have been wrong and give me humility to admit it. Take pride, defensiveness and the need to win out of my heart. Give me a soft answer, a listening ear and courage to speak the truth in love.\n\nMove in the heart of the other person. Soften what is hard. Open a door to talk. Bring healing where there is hurt. And if reconciliation is not yet possible, keep me from bitterness and keep me at peace as far as it depends on me. Make me a peacemaker, called a child of God. In Jesus' name, Amen."},{"title":"Father, protect our unity","text":"Lord, You prayed that we may be one. I ask You to protect the unity of my family, my church and my workplace. Expose every whisper, division and offence that Satan uses.\n\nWhere I have spoken carelessly, forgive me. Where I have been hurt, heal me. Give me the grace to forgive quickly and to ask forgiveness quickly. Help me to see people as You see them, as people for whom Christ died.\n\nLet Your peace rule in our hearts. Let us bear with one another in love, and let the world see Christ in how we treat each other. In Jesus' name, Amen."}],"declare":["Blessed are the peacemakers; I am a child of God.","I speak the truth in love.","I forgive quickly and I seek peace.","Christ is my peace."]},"salvation":{"doc":["Salvation is the heart of the Gospel, and it is a gift. Ephesians 2:8-9 says, \"For by grace are ye saved through faith; and that not of yourselves: it is the gift of God: not of works, lest any man should boast.\" The Greek charis (grace) is unearned favour, and doron (gift) is something given freely. You cannot buy it, earn it or deserve it. You can only receive it.","Why do we need it? Romans 3:23 says, \"all have sinned, and come short of the glory of God.\" Romans 6:23 adds, \"the wages of sin is death; but the gift of God is eternal life through Jesus Christ our Lord.\" Sin is not only bad behaviour, it is a break in relationship with the holy God, and the penalty is separation from Him. No amount of good deeds can repair it. That is why God Himself acted.","The cross is God's answer. 2 Corinthians 5:21 says, \"He hath made him to be sin for us, who knew no sin; that we might be made the righteousness of God in him.\" This is the great exchange: our sin to Him, His righteousness to us. Romans 5:8 says God shows His love in that Christ died for us while we were still sinners. The resurrection proves that the payment was accepted and that death is defeated (1 Corinthians 15:3-4, 20).","How do we receive it? Romans 10:9-10 says, \"if thou shalt confess with thy mouth the Lord Jesus, and shalt believe in thine heart that God hath raised him from the dead, thou shalt be saved.\" Repent, which means turn around, and believe. The Greek sozo means to save, rescue and make whole. Salvation has a past (we were saved), a present (we are being saved) and a future (we will be saved). It begins a new life, led by the Spirit, lived in community, and growing in obedience. Assurance comes from God's promise: \"These things have I written unto you that believe on the name of the Son of God; that ye may know that ye have eternal life\" (1 John 5:13)."],"christ":"Revealed in Christ: the name Jesus is the Greek form of Yeshua, which means \"the Lord saves\" (Matthew 1:21). He is not only the teacher of the way, He is the way (John 14:6). \"Neither is there salvation in any other: for there is none other name under heaven given among men, whereby we must be saved\" (Acts 4:12).","word":"The word behind the word: sozo is the same verb used for forgiven, healed, delivered and rescued from drowning. When Peter sank and cried, \"Lord, save me\" (Matthew 14:30), he used sozo. Salvation is that cry answered by a hand.","walk":"Walk it out: If you have never given your life to Christ, you can do it now, with a simple honest prayer. If you have, thank Him, and tell one person this week what He has done for you. Find a church or fellowship to grow in, and read the Gospel of John.","scriptures":[{"ref":"Ephesians 2:8-9","text":"For by grace are ye saved through faith; and that not of yourselves: it is the gift of God: Not of works, lest any man should boast."},{"ref":"John 3:16-17","text":"For God so loved the world, that he gave his only begotten Son, that whosoever believeth in him should not perish, but have everlasting life. For God sent not his Son into the world to condemn the world; but that the world through him might be saved."},{"ref":"Romans 10:9-13","text":"That if thou shalt confess with thy mouth the Lord Jesus, and shalt believe in thine heart that God hath raised him from the dead, thou shalt be saved. For with the heart man believeth unto righteousness; and with the mouth confession is made unto salvation. For the scripture saith, Whosoever believeth on him shall not be ashamed. For there is no difference between the Jew and the Greek: for the same Lord over all is rich unto all that call upon him. For whosoever shall call upon the name of the Lord shall be saved."},{"ref":"Romans 6:23","text":"For the wages of sin is death; but the gift of God is eternal life through Jesus Christ our Lord."},{"ref":"Acts 4:12","text":"Neither is there salvation in any other: for there is none other name under heaven given among men, whereby we must be saved."},{"ref":"2 Corinthians 5:17-21","text":"Therefore if any man be in Christ, he is a new creature: old things are passed away; behold, all things are become new. And all things are of God, who hath reconciled us to himself by Jesus Christ, and hath given to us the ministry of reconciliation; To wit, that God was in Christ, reconciling the world unto himself, not imputing their trespasses unto them; and hath committed unto us the word of reconciliation. Now then we are ambassadors for Christ, as though God did beseech you by us: we pray you in Christ's stead, be ye reconciled to God. For he hath made him to be sin for us, who knew no sin; that we might be made the righteousness of God in him."},{"ref":"1 John 5:11-13","text":"And this is the record, that God hath given to us eternal life, and this life is in his Son. He that hath the Son hath life; and he that hath not the Son of God hath not life. These things have I written unto you that believe on the name of the Son of God; that ye may know that ye have eternal life, and that ye may believe on the name of the Son of God."}],"prayers":[{"title":"A prayer to receive Christ","text":"Lord Jesus, I come to You as I am. I confess that I have sinned and fallen short of Your glory. I am sorry, and I turn from my sins. I believe that You are the Son of God, that You died on the cross for my sins, and that God raised You from the dead.\n\nI receive Your forgiveness. I invite You to be my Saviour and my Lord. Come into my life, make me new, fill me with Your Holy Spirit. Write my name in the book of life. Thank You that I am a child of God, forgiven and loved, not by my works but by Your grace.\n\nHelp me to follow You every day. Lead me to Your Word and to Your people. Give me courage to tell others. I give You my life. In Jesus' name, Amen."},{"title":"Thank You for saving me","text":"Father, I thank You for the gift of salvation. Thank You that while I was far from You, Christ died for me. Thank You that I was lost and am found, blind and now see, dead and now alive.\n\nHelp me to live worthy of this gift, not to earn it but to honour it. Keep me from going back to my old ways. Where I doubt my salvation, assure me by Your Word and Your Spirit. Where I am weak, lift me.\n\nMake me a witness. Bring to my mind the people who do not know You, and give me love, words and opportunities. Let the whole earth hear of the Saviour. All glory to You, Father, Son and Holy Spirit, now and forever. In Jesus' name, Amen."}],"declare":["I am saved by grace through faith.","Jesus Christ is my Lord and Saviour.","I have passed from death to life.","Nothing can snatch me out of the Father's hand."]}};
